----------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Inductive step).  \n\nAssume that for some integer \\(m\\ge3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_m^{(k)}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen for the integer \\(n=m+1\\) the following two statements hold:\n\n1. For every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. For every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k+1)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(n=m+1\\).  The set \\(S_n\\) consists of the points of \\(S_m\\) together with the single new point \\((n,1)\\) (because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\) when \\(a=n\\)).  \n\n1. **Preserving the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L_m^{(k)}\\) be a family for \\(S_m\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Add the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{vert}}:\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   This line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  It contains the new point \\((n,1)\\) and no point of \\(S_m\\).  Because none of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m^{(k)}\\) contains a point with \\(x=n\\), the line \\(L_{\\text{vert}}\\) is distinct from all of them.  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}:=\\mathcal L_m^{(k)}\\cup\\{L_{\\text{vert}}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   is a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and still having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. **Increasing the number of sunny lines by one.**  \n   Again start from \\(\\mathcal L_m^{(k)}\\).  Add the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{sun}}:\\;y=x-(n-1) .\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope equals \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny.  The line passes through \\((n,1)\\) and also through the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lceil\\tfrac{n}{2}\\rceil,\\; \\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\rfloor+1\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   all of which belong to \\(S_m\\) and are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_m^{(k)}\\).  Since none of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m^{(k)}\\) contains the point \\((n,1)\\), the line \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) is new.  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n}^{(k+1)}:=\\mathcal L_m^{(k)}\\cup\\{L_{\\text{sun}}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   is a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Induction).  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe statement is true for \\(n=3\\) by Lemma 1.  \nAssume it holds for some \\(n-1\\ge3\\).  By Lemma 2, from a family for \\(S_{n-1}\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain a family for \\(S_n\\) with either \\(k\\) or \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  Starting from the families for \\(S_{n-1}\\) that realize all numbers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\), we therefore obtain families for \\(S_n\\) that realize all numbers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).  This completes the induction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final Answer  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
