done: the \\(n\\) vertical lines already cover all of \\(T_n\\).\n\nOtherwise \\(m\\le n-1\\) and we put \\(r=n-m=k\\).  \nApply Lemma 2 with this \\(r\\); we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) covering the\nremaining points \\(S_{k}=T_n\\setminus H_m\\).\n\nThus we have constructed exactly \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, altogether\n\\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completeness of the answer\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); consequently all these\nvalues are attainable.\n\nConversely, no value \\(k>n\\) can occur because we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, so at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny.  \nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Result**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{, the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly }k=0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
