s \\((p,0)\\), the \\(y\\)-axis \\((0,q)\\) or the line \\(x+y=0\\) \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nFor any integer \\(n\\ge1\\) a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\). Moreover the bound (1) is attained.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with rational slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nLet \\(m=\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) be reduced, \\(q>0\\).  \nAll integer points on the line have abscissas congruent to a fixed residue modulo \\(q\\); consequently the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(T_n\\) on the line differ by multiples of \\(q\\).  \nSince \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) such abscissas can occur.  \nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nIf \\(q=1\\) the slope is an integer \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nFrom the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\((m+1)a+c\\le n+1\\).  \nSince \\(|m+1|\\ge2\\), the number of admissible integer values of \\(a\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{|m+1|}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nThus any sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points.\n\nThe bound is sharp:  \n\n* if \\(n\\) is odd, \\(m=1\\) and \\(c=0\\) give the line \\(y=x\\), which contains the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2},\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr)\\) \u2013 exactly \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}=f(n)\\) points;  \n\n* if \\(n\\) is even, \\(m=-2\\) and \\(c=n+2\\) give the line \\(y=-2x+n+2\\), which contains the points \\((1,n),(2,n-2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\frac{n}{2},2\\bigr)\\) \u2013 exactly \\(\\frac{n}{2}=f(n)\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound \\(k\\le3\\)\n\nConsider any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\nA sunny line is not vertical; therefore it meets each vertical line \\(x=a\\) in **at most one** point.\nConsequently, to cover the points of the first \\(n-3\\) columns  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-3\\},\n\\]\n\nwe need at least \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the optimal choice is the vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\)).  \nAfter removing those \\(n-3\\) columns the uncovered points are exactly\n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nthe triangular set for \\(n=3\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1, a sunny line can contain at most \\(f(3)=\\lfloor\\frac{4}{2}\\rfloor=2\\) points of \\(T_3\\); therefore at least three sunny lines are required to cover the six points of \\(T_3\\).  \n\nHence any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines uses **at most three sunny lines**, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of configurations for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), coverings attaining each admissible value of \\(k\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n  \\]\n\n  All are non\u2011sunny and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); cover it by any sunny line, e.g.  \n\n  \\[\n  y=\\tfrac12x+\\Bigl(1-\\tfrac12n\\Bigr).\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n  Cover each of them by a distinct sunny line (any line through a single point with a sunny slope suffices).\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n  The six uncovered points form the set \\(T_3\\).  \n  They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\