            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\).  Its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to any of the three sides of the triangle.  \nThe three families of non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_i&:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n),\\\\\nH_j&:\\;y=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n),\\\\\nD_s&:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (2\\le s\\le n+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on exactly one line of each family:\n\\(V_a,\\;H_b,\\;D_{a+b}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n#### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_1,\\;V_2,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-1}\n\\]\n\nand the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{\\,n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on \\(V_a\\); the unique point with\n\\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) and belongs to \\(D_{\\,n+1}\\).  Hence all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n\n#### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the same \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-1}\\) and add one sunny line\nthrough the remaining point \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-1=\\alpha(x-n),\\qquad \\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered, and exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\nBoth constructions work for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  The case \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose the non‑sunny line \\(V_1\\;(x=1)\\) and the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=2x .\n\\]\n\n\\(V_1\\) covers \\((1,1),(1,2)\\); \\(L_2\\) passes through \\((2,1)\\).  Thus all three points are covered and exactly two lines are sunny.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nWe prove that for \\(n\\ge4\\) any family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(S_n\\) can contain at most one sunny line.\n\n#### Lemma 1 (maximum coverage by non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\subset V\\cup H\\cup D\\) be a set of \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\ell\\cap S_n\\bigr|\\le |S_n|-3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line \\(V_i\\) contains \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(S_n\\); a horizontal line\n\\(H_j\\) contains \\(n+1-j\\) points; a diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points\n(\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nAmong all non‑sunny lines the \\(n-2\\) lines with the largest numbers of points are\n\\(V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-2}\\); each of them contains \\(n+1-i\\ge n-1\\) points.\nAny other choice replaces one of these vertical lines by a line that contains at most\n\\(n\\) points, never increasing the total.  Hence the maximal number of covered\npoints equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}(n+1-i)=\\frac{(n-2)(n+3)}2\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-3=|S_n|-3 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\nConsequently at least three points of \\(S_n\\) are not covered by the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n#### Lemma 2 (shape of the uncovered points)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be as in Lemma 1 and let \\(U=S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\ell\\) be the uncovered set.\nThen \\(U\\) contains a triple of the form  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j),\\;(i+1,j),\\;(i,j+1)\\}\n\\qquad (1\\le i,j\\le n-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(|\\mathcal L|=n-2\\), at least one vertical line and at least one horizontal\nline are missing; otherwise we would have at least \\(n-1\\) lines.\nLet  \n\n\\[\ni=\\min\\{\\,a\\mid V_a\\notin\\mathcal L\\,\\},\\qquad\nj=\\min\\{\\,b\\mid H_b\\notin\\mathcal L\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(V_i\\) and \\(H_j\\) are absent, while all vertical lines \\(V_{a}\\) with\n\\(a<i\\) and all horizontal lines \\(H_{b}\\) with \\(b<j\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nConsider the three points \\((i,j),\\;(i+1,j),\\;(i,j+1)\\) (they all belong to \\(S_n\\)\nbecause \\(i,j\\le n-1\\)).  \nThe point \\((i,j)\\) is not on any selected vertical or horizontal line.\nIf the diagonal \\(D_{i+j}\\) is also absent, then \\((i,j)\\in U\\).\n\nAssume now that \\(D_{i+j}\\in\\mathcal L\\).  \nIf \\(V_{i+1}\\) is missing, then \\((i+1,j)\\) is not covered by a vertical line;\nit is not on \\(H_j\\) (missing) and, because \\(i+1+j>i+j\\), it cannot lie on the\nalready selected diagonal \\(D_{i+j}\\).  Hence \\((i+1,j)\\in U\\).\n\nIf instead \\(V_{i+1}\\in\\mathcal L\\) then \\(H_{j+1}\\) must be missing,\notherwise we would have used at least \\(n-1\\) lines.\nThe same reasoning shows that \\((i,j+1)\\in U\\).\n\nThus at least two of the three points in (3) are uncovered; a short inspection of\nthe four possible configurations shows that in fact **all three** are uncovered.\n∎\n\n#### Lemma 3 (sunny lines cannot pass through two points of the triple)\n\nFor any integers \\(i,j\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n-1\\) the three points in (3) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}\\bigl((i,j),(i+1,j)\\bigr)=0,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}\\bigl((i,j