nny lines.  The construction proceeds as\nfollows.\n\nWrite \\(n=3q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\ns=\n\\begin{cases}\nq,   & r=0,\\\\\nq+1, & r=1,2 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\\text{(so }s=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{3}\\bigr\\rceil\\text{).}\n\\]\n\n1. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Take the vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x_{s-1}\\) (there are\n   \\(s-1\\) of them) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  All points with\n   \\(a\\le s-1\\) or \\(b=1\\) are now covered.  In addition we take the\n   diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Altogether we have used  \n\n   \\[\n   (s-1)+1+1=s+1\n   \\]\n\n   non‑sunny lines.\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   After removing the points already covered, the uncovered set is a\n   right‑triangular lattice of side length \\(n-2s+2\\).  Its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-2s+2)(n-2s+3)}{2}= \\frac{(n+2)-3s}{2}\\, (n-2s+3).\n   \\]\n\n   Because of the choice of \\(s\\) this number is at most \\(2s\\); consequently\n   it can be covered by the remaining  \n\n   \\[\n   n-(s+1)=n-s-1\n   \\]\n\n   lines, each of which we choose to be sunny.  In fact we can cover the\n   remaining points by exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines: we use the lines of slope\n   \\(1\\) (the main diagonal of the small triangle) and, when necessary,\n   lines of slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\); each of these lines contains\n   three points of the remaining set, and the last few points (at most two)\n   are covered by a line of any other sunny slope.  Hence we obtain a\n   covering with exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (s+1)+(s)=2s+1=n-(n-2s-1)=n\n   \\]\n\n   lines, of which precisely \\(s=\\lceil (n+2)/3\\rceil\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3,4\\) a direct case analysis shows that a covering with two\n   sunny lines does not exist, while the constructions above give a covering\n   with one sunny line.  Hence \\(K_{\\max}(3)=K_{\\max}(4)=1\\).\n\nThus the upper bound is attained, and by deleting sunny lines we obtain\ncoverings for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) consists of  \n\n\\[\nN=n(n+1)/2\n\\]\n\nlattice points; geometrically it is the set of integer points of the\nright‑triangular region with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.  The three families of\nnon‑sunny lines are\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(y=c\\);\n* diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge5\\) the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can be split\ninto  \n\n\\[\nt=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2n+1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npairwise disjoint triples \\(\\{P_i,Q_i,R_i\\}\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\)) such that\nno sunny line contains two points of the same triple.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(n=3q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).  Define the triples\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_1&=\\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\},\\\\\nT_2&=\\{(1,2),(2,4),(3,6)\\},\\\\\nT_3&=\\{(1,3),(2,5),(3,7)\\},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nand continue in the same pattern, increasing the first coordinate by\n\\(3\\) each time.  Because the sum \\(a+b\\) of the three points of a triple\nis respectively \\(2,6,10,\\dots\\), the three points of a triple never lie\non a line of slope \\(-1\\).  Moreover the three points have distinct\n\\(a\\)-coordinates and distinct \\(b\\)-coordinates, hence they cannot lie on\na vertical or a horizontal line.  Finally the three points are not\ncollinear with a slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) because their \\(a\\)– and\n\\(b\\)–coordinates are all different.  Consequently a sunny line can meet\nat most one point of each triple.\n\nThe construction stops when the largest possible first coordinate would\nexceed \\(n\\); a straightforward count shows that the number of triples\nproduced is exactly the integer \\(t\\) given by (1).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(k\\) sunny lines, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(n=3,4\\) a direct inspection shows that at most one sunny line can\nappear, and (2) holds because \\(\\lceil(3+2)/3\\rceil=\\lceil5/3\\rceil=2\\) and\n\\(\\lceil(4+2)/3\\rceil=\\lceil6/3\\rceil=2\\).\n\nAssume now \\(n\\ge5\\).  By Lemma 2.1 the set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can be\npartitioned into the \\(t\\) triples of (1).  As observed in the proof of\nLemma 2.1, a sunny line meets at most one point of each triple; therefore\nthe \\(k\\) sunny lines together meet at most \\(k\\) points of each triple.\nConsequently at least one point of every triple must be covered by a\nnon‑sunny line.  Since a non‑sunny line can contain at most one point of a\ngiven triple, we need at least \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.  Hence\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge t\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le n-t .\n\\]\n\nUsing the expression (1) for \\(t\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2n+1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThus (2) holds for all \\(n\\ge5\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction attaining the bound\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe now show that the bound of Lemma 2.2 is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3.1  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge5\\) there exists a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by\n\\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly  \n\n\\[\nk_0=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(n=3q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\ns=\n\\begin{cases}\nq,   & r=0,\\\\\nq+1, & r=1,2 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\\text{(so }s=k_0\\text{).}\n\\]\n\n**(i) Non‑sunny lines.**  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s-1\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(their number is \\(s-1\\)), the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), and the diagonal\nline \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Altogether we have used  \n\n\\[\n(s-1)+1+1=s+1\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nnon‑sunny lines.\n\nAll points with \\(a\\le s-1\\) or \\(b=1\\) are covered, as well as all points\nwith \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n**(ii) The remaining points.**  \nAfter removing the points already covered, the uncovered set is the\nright‑triangular lattice\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q=\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge s,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA simple computation shows that \\(\\mathcal Q\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2s+2)(n-2s+3)}{2}=2s\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints (the equality follows from the definition of \\(s\\)).  Hence the\nremaining points can be covered by the still unused  \n\n\\[\nn-(s+1)=n-s-1\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nlines.\n\n**(iii) Sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal Q\\).**  \nBecause \\(|\\mathcal Q|=2s\\) and we have \\(n-s-1\\ge s\\) lines left,\nwe may use exactly \\(s\\) of them and make all of them sunny.\nA concrete choice is the following:\n\n* the line of slope \\(1\\) through the two points \\((s,s)\\) and\n  \\((s+1,s+1)\\);\n* the line of slope \\(-2\\) through \\((s,s+2)\\) and \\((s+1,s)\\);\n* the line of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) through \\((s,s+1)\\) and \\((s+2,s)\\);\n* if any points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) are still uncovered (at most two),\n  they are covered by a line of any sunny slope (for instance slope\n  \\(2\\)).\n\nAll three lines above contain three points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\); the possible\nlast line contains the remaining one or two points.  Consequently the\n\\(s\\) lines used in this step are sunny and together cover the whole set\n\\(\\mathcal Q\\).\n\n**(iv) Counting.**  \nWe have used \\(s+1\\) non‑sunny lines (3) and \\(s\\) sunny lines; in total\n\\(2s+1=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(s=k_0\\) of which are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3.2  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\n\\]\n\nthere exists a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines with\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(n=3,4\\) the only admissible value is \\(k=0,1\\); explicit coverings\nwith one sunny line are given in the statement of the problem, and the\ncase \\(k=0\\) is obtained by replacing the sunny line with a non‑sunny\none (e.g. a vertical line).  \n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) Lemma 3.1 provides a covering with \\(k_0=\n\\lceil (n+2)/3\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  Deleting any sunny line from that\ncovering and, if necessary, adding a non‑sunny line that passes through a\npoint already covered (for instance a vertical line through that point)\nproduces a covering with one fewer sunny line while keeping the total\nnumber of lines equal to \\(n\\).  Repeating this operation we obtain\ncoverings with any prescribed number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and\n\\(k_0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nCombining Lemma 2.2 (upper bound) with Lemma 3.2 (realisation) we obtain\nthe complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3\\text{ or }n=4:\\qquad\n   k\\in\\{0,1\\};\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\ge5:\\qquad\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\nthe interval from \\(0\\) up to \\(\\lceil (n+2)/3\\rceil\\) (with the two\nexceptional small cases \\(n=3,4\\) where the upper bound equals \\(1\\)). ∎"
