------------------------------------\nWe now show that the bound of Lemma\u202f2.2 is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3.1  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge5\\) there exists a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by\n\\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly  \n\n\\[\nk_0=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(n=3q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\ns=\n\\begin{cases}\nq,   & r=0,\\\\\nq+1, & r=1,2 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\\text{(so }s=k_0\\text{).}\n\\]\n\n**(i) Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=s-1\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(their number is \\(s-1\\)), the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), and the diagonal\nline \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Altogether we have used  \n\n\\[\n(s-1)+1+1=s+1\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nnon\u2011sunny lines.\n\nAll points with \\(a\\le s-1\\) or \\(b=1\\) are covered, as well as all points\nwith \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n**(ii) The remaining points.**  \nAfter removing the points already covered, the uncovered set is the\nright\u2011triangular lattice\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q=\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge s,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA simple computation shows that \\(\\mathcal Q\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2s+2)(n-2s+3)}{2}=2s\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints (the equality follows from the definition of \\(s\\)).  Hence the\nremaining points can be covered by the still unused  \n\n\\[\nn-(s+1)=n-s-1\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nlines.\n\n**(iii) Sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal Q\\).**  \nBecause \\(|\\mathcal Q|=2s\\) and we have \\(n-s-1\\ge s\\) lines left,\nwe may use exactly \\(s\\) of them and make all of them sunny.\nA concrete choice is the following:\n\n* the line of slope \\(1\\) through the two points \\((s,s)\\) and\n  \\((s+1,s+1)\\);\n* the line of slope \\(-2\\) through \\((s,s+2)\\) and \\((s+1,s)\\);\n* the line of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) through \\((s,s+1)\\) and \\((s+2,s)\\);\n* if any points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) are still uncovered (at most two),\n  they are covered by a line of any sunny slope (for instance slope\n  \\(2\\)).\n\nAll three lines above contain three points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\); the possible\nlast line contains the remaining one or two points.  Consequently the\n\\(s\\) lines used in this step are sunny and together cover the whole set\n\\(\\mathcal Q\\).\n\n**(iv) Counting.**  \nWe have used \\(s+1\\) non\u2011sunny lines (3) and \\(s\\) sunny lines; in total\n\\(2s+1=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(s=k_0\\) of which are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3.2  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\n\\]\n\nthere exists a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines with\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(n=3,4\\) the only admissible value is \\(k=0,1\\); explicit coverings\nwith one sunny line are given in the statement of the problem, and the\ncase \\(k=0\\) is obtained by replacing the sunny line with a non\u2011sunny\none (e.g. a vertical line).  \n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) Lemma\u202f3.1 provides a covering with \\(k_0=\n\\lceil (n+2)/3\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  Deleting any sunny line from that\ncovering and, if necessary, adding a non\u2011sunny line that passes through a\npoint already covered (for instance a vertical line through that point)\nproduces a covering with one fewer sunny line while keeping the total\nnumber of lines equal to \\(n\\).  Repeating this operation we obtain\ncoverings with any prescribed number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and\n\\(k_0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f2.2 (upper bound) with Lemma\u202f3.2 (realisation) we obtain\nthe complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3\\text{ or }n=4:\\qquad\n   k\\in\\{0,1\\};\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\ge5:\\qquad\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\nthe interval from \\(0\\) up to \\(\\lceil (n+2)/3\\rceil\\) (with the two\nexceptional small cases \\(n=3,4\\) where the upper bound equals \\(1\\)). \u220e"
