            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector \\((p,q)\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p+q\\neq0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor any \\(n\\ge1\\) a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\). Moreover the bound (1) is attained.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with rational slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nLet \\(m=p/q\\) be reduced, \\(q>0\\).  \nAll integer points on the line have abscissas congruent to a fixed residue modulo \\(q\\); therefore the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(T_n\\) on the line differ by multiples of \\(q\\).  \nSince \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) such abscissas can occur.  \nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n\nIf \\(q=1\\) the slope is an integer \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\((m+1)a+c\\le n+1\\).  \nBecause \\(|m+1|\\ge2\\), the number of admissible integer values of \\(a\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n\nThus any sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points.\n\nThe bound is sharp:  \n\n* if \\(n\\) is odd, the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) contains the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2},\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr)\\), i.e. \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}=f(n)\\) points;  \n\n* if \\(n\\) is even, the line \\(y=-2x+n+2\\) (slope \\(-2\\)) contains the points \\((1,n),(2,n-2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\frac{n}{2},2\\bigr)\\), i.e. \\(\\frac{n}{2}=f(n)\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that \\(T_n\\) is covered by \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which \\(s\\) are sunny and \\(t=n-s\\) are non‑sunny.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most \\(f(n)\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\nvertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)) or the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).  \nIn each case the line meets the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in at most \\(n-1\\) lattice points (the longest possible intersection is a column or a row of length \\(n-1\\)).  \nHence every non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered satisfies\n\n\\[\ns\\,f(n)+(n-s)(n-1)\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-6\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(the six points of the last three columns form the set \\(T_3\\); removing them leaves the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-6\\) points).\n\nBecause \\(f(n)\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), inequality (2) implies\n\n\\[\ns\\frac{n+1}{2}+(n-s)(n-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2-6 .\n\\]\n\nAfter rearrangement we obtain\n\n\\[\n(n-8)s\\le (n-3)(n-8).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Case \\(n\\ge8\\).*  \nHere \\(n-8\\ge0\\), so (4) forces \\(s\\le3\\).\n\n*Case \\(3\\le n\\le7\\).*  \nWe check (2) directly for \\(s=4\\).  \nUsing the exact value \\(f(n)=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) we compute\n\n\\[\nU:=4\\,f(n)+(n-4)(n-1)\n\\]\n\nand compare with \\(|T_n|\\) from (3):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nn & U & |T_n|\\\\\\hline\n3 & 4\\cdot2+(-1)\\cdot2=6 & 0\\\\\n4 & 4\\cdot2+0\\cdot3=8 & 4\\\\\n5 & 4\\cdot3+1\\cdot4=16 & 9\\\\\n6 & 4\\cdot3+2\\cdot5=26 & 15\\\\\n7 & 4\\cdot4+3\\cdot6=36 & 22\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIn each case \\(U<|T_n|\\), contradicting (2).  \nThus \\(s\\ge4\\) is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nTherefore **any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines uses at most three sunny lines**, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions attaining \\(k=0,1,2,3\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), coverings with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for each admissible \\(k\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n  \\]\n\n  All are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); cover it by any sunny line, e.g.  \n\n  \\[\n  y=\\tfrac12x+\\Bigl(1-\\tfrac12n\\Bigr).\n  \\]\n\n* \\(k=2\\).  \n  Use the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n  Cover each by a distinct sunny line (any line through a single point with a sunny slope works).\n\n* \\(k=3\\).  \n  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n  The six points that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n  \\[\n  T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;x+2y=5,\n  \\]\n\n  whose slopes are \\(1,\\,-2,\\,-\\tfrac12\\); none is \\(0,\\