(T_n\\) lie on it.\nThe same bound holds for horizontal lines and for lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)\n(their equation is \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\), giving \\(d-1\\le n\\) points).\nHence each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(f(n)\\) points, where \\(f(n)\\) is given by (5).\n\nTherefore the covering must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot n+s\\cdot f(n)\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n(n-s)n+s f(n)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSolving (8) for the smallest possible \\(t\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nt\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{|T_n|-n f(n)}{\\,n-f(n)} .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) and the explicit value of \\(f(n)\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) is even:* \\(f(n)=m\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{m(2m+1)-2m\\cdot m}{2m-m}\n      =\\frac{m}{m}=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus at least one non‑sunny line is necessary, i.e.  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd:* \\(f(n)=m+1\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{(2m+1)(m+1)-(2m+1)(m+1)}{(2m+1)-(m+1)}=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence no non‑sunny line is forced; all \\(n\\) lines may be sunny:\n\n\\[\ns\\le n .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nConsequently any admissible covering must satisfy (10) when \\(n\\) is even and (11) when \\(n\\) is odd. These are the **necessary** restrictions on \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying the necessary bound:\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\qquad\n0\\le k\\le n-1\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\]\n\nSet \\(t=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines we shall use).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(t\\) lines are distinct, non‑sunny, and together cover all points of\n   \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   The points not yet covered are  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge t+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(a'=a-(t+1)\\) (so \\(a'\\ge0\\)). Then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes\n   \\(a'+b\\le n-t\\). Hence \\(U\\) is exactly the translate by \\((t+1,0)\\) of the set\n   \\(T_{n-t}\\).\n\n3. **Sunny covering of \\(U\\).**  \n   By Lemma 1 the set \\(T_{n-t}\\) can be covered by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|T_{n-t}|}{f(n-t)}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =k\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines (the equality follows from (9), which is an equality when we\n   substitute \\(t=n-k\\)).  \n   An explicit family is as follows.\n\n   *If \\(n-t\\) is odd, write \\(n-t=2r+1\\).  \n   Use the \\(r+1\\) lines*\n   \\[\n   y=x+c\\qquad(c=0,-1,-2,\\dots , -r).\n   \\]\n   *Each such line contains exactly two points of the translated triangle,\n   and together they cover all its points.*\n\n   *If \\(n-t\\) is even, write \\(n-t=2r\\).  \n   Use the \\(r\\) lines*\n   \\[\n   y=-2x+d\\qquad(d=n-t+2,\\;n-t,\\;n-t-2,\\dots ,4).\n   \\]\n   *Again each line contains two points and the whole triangle is covered.*\n\n   Translating these lines back by \\((t+1,0)\\) yields \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that\n   cover precisely the set \\(U\\).\n\nAll together we have \\(t\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines,\nexactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of\nthem.  By varying \\(k\\) within the allowed range we obtain a covering for every\nadmissible number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines in a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\qquad\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is even}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nBoth the necessity (counting argument) and the sufficiency (explicit\nconstruction) have been proved rigorously."
