\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   n\\cdot n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality is always true, and it imposes **no restriction** on \\(s\\).  \n   Hence any integer \\(0\\le s\\le n\\) (i.e. any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)) is *a priori* possible.  \n   This establishes the **necessary** part of the statement.\n\n4. **Sufficient construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(t=n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the \\(t\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining set.*  \n   The points not yet covered are  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge t+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Writing \\(a'=a-(t+1)\\) (so \\(a'\\ge0\\)) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   a'+b\\le n-t .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(U\\) is exactly the translate by \\((t+1,0)\\) of the triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n-t}=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le n-t+1\\}.\n   \\tag{*}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 3 – covering \\(U\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   Because \\(k=n-t\\), the set \\(T_{n-t}\\) contains \\(\\frac{(n-t)(n-t+1)}2\\) points, while a sunny line can contain up to \\(n-t\\) of them (Lemma 1).  \n   Therefore it is always possible to cover \\(T_{n-t}\\) with exactly \\(n-t\\) sunny lines: simply take, for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-t\\), the line  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -c\\,x + (n-t+1) .\n   \\tag{L_c}\n   \\]\n\n   The direction vector of \\(L_c\\) is \\((1,-c)\\); since \\(c\\ge1\\) we have \\(p=1\\neq0\\), \\(q=-c\\neq0\\) and \\(p+q=1-c\\neq0\\). Thus each \\(L_c\\) is sunny.  \n   For a fixed \\(c\\) the points of \\(L_c\\) inside \\(*\\) are precisely the pairs \\((x,y)\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -c x + (n-t+1),\\qquad x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1,\\;x+y\\le n-t+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   One checks that for each \\(c\\) there is at least one such lattice point, and different values of \\(c\\) give distinct lines. Moreover the collection \\(\\{L_c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-t\\}\\) covers every point of \\(*\\): given \\((x,y)\\in *\\) set \\(c = n-t+1 - (x+y)\\) (which lies between \\(1\\) and \\(n-t\\) because \\(2\\le x+y\\le n-t+1\\)); then \\((x,y)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_c\\).\n\n   Translating each \\(L_c\\) by the vector \\((t+1,0)\\) yields the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde L_c:\\; y = -c\\,(x-(t+1)) + (n-t+1),\\qquad c=1,\\dots ,n-t .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(k=n-t\\) lines are distinct, sunny, and together cover exactly the set \\(U\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – the whole covering.*  \n   The family consisting of the \\(t\\) vertical lines \\(L_i\\) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) contains \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\). By construction exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Because the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), the sufficient condition is proved.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   No restriction other than \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) follows from the problem data, and the explicit construction shows that every such \\(k\\) can indeed be realised. Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\), and this bound is attainable.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(px+qy=r\\) where \\(p,q\\) are coprime integers, \\(q>0\\).  \nThe line is sunny iff  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p+q\\neq0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) are two lattice points on the line, then  \n\n\\[\np(x_1-x_2)+q(y_1-y_2)=0\\Longrightarrow (x_1-x_2,y_1-y_2)=k(q,-p)\n\\]\n\nfor some non‑zero integer \\(k\\). Hence moving from one lattice point to the next changes the sum \\(x+y\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta (x+y)=k(q-p).\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2) we have \\(q-p\\neq0\\); consequently \\(|\\Delta (x+y)|\\ge1\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\); therefore along a given line the quantity \\(x+y\\) can assume at most \\(n\\) different integer values, and consequently the line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe bound is sharp: the line \\(x+2y=r\\) (direction vector \\((1,2)\\)) satisfies \\(|q-p|=1\\); as \\(k\\) varies, the sum \\(x+y\\) runs through every integer between its minimal and maximal admissible values, giving exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (e.g. the vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\)).  \n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence the total number of covered points satisfies  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot n+s\\cdot n\\ge |T_n| .\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn^2\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds for every \\(n\\ge1\\). No inequality involving \\(s\\) (or \\(k\\)) is forced; therefore the only necessary restriction is  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k=s\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficient construction\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and set \\(t=n-k\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,t).\n   \\]\n\n   They are pairwise distinct, non‑sunny, and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is at most \\(t\\).\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   The uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge t+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Writing \\(a'=a-(t+1)\\) we have \\(a'\\ge0\\) and the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   a'+b\\le n-t .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(U\\) is the translate by \\((t+1,0)\\) of the triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n-t}=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le n-t+1\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny covering of \\(U\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-t\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde L_c:\\; y = -c\\bigl(x-(t+1)\\bigr)+(n-t+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Its direction vector is \\((1,-c)\\); because \\(c\\ge1\\) we have \\(p=1\\neq0\\), \\(q=-c\\neq0\\) and \\(p+q=1-c\\neq0\\), so \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) is sunny.\n\n   Let \\((x,y)\\in U\\). Put \\(c=n-t+1-(x+y)\\). Since \\(2\\le x+y\\le n-t+1\\), we have \\(1\\le c\\le n-t\\), and a direct substitution shows that \\((x,y)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\widetilde L_c\\). Hence every point of \\(U\\) lies on one of the \\(\\widetilde L_c\\).\n\n   Distinct values of \\(c\\) give distinct lines, so we obtain exactly \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines covering \\(U\\).\n\n4. **Complete family.**  \n   The collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}= \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde L_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nSince the construction works for any admissible \\(k\\), the sufficient condition matches (3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\; } .\n\\]"
