ger values, and the line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe bound is sharp.  Consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;x+2y=r .\n\\]\n\nHere \\((p,q)=(1,2)\\); thus \\(\\Delta (x+y)=|2-1|=1\\).  As \\(k\\) varies, the sum \\(x+y\\) runs through every integer between its minimal and maximal admissible values, giving exactly one lattice point of \\(T_n\\) for each value of the sum.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) contains precisely \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (e.g. the vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\)).  \nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line also meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot n+s\\cdot n\\ge |T_n| .\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn^2\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds for every \\(n\\ge1\\).  No inequality involving \\(s\\) follows; therefore any integer  \n\n\\[\n0\\le s\\le n\n\\]\n\nis **necessary**.  (We write \\(k\\) for \\(s\\) in the statement of the problem.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficient construction\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k .\n\\]\n\n#### 1.  Non‑sunny lines\nTake the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct, non‑sunny, and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\n#### 2.  The uncovered set\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge t+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a'=a-(t+1)\\) (so \\(a'\\ge0\\)).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na'+b\\le n-t . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a'+t+1,b)\\mid a',b\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\;b\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le n-t\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(U\\) is the translate by the vector \\((t+1,0)\\) of the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-t}^\\ast=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\times\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid x+y\\le n-t\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(The first coordinate is allowed to be \\(0\\); this avoids the difficulty noted in the bug report.)\n\n#### 3.  Sunny covering of \\(U\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_c:\\; y = -c\\bigl(x-(t+1)\\bigr)+(n-t+1). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector of \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) is \\((1,-c)\\); because \\(c\\ge1\\) we have \\(p=1\\neq0,\\;q=-c\\neq0,\\;p+q=1-c\\neq0\\).  Hence every \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) is sunny.\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in U\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\nc = n-t+1-(a+b). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n-t+1\\); therefore \\(1\\le c\\le k\\).  Substituting this value of \\(c\\) into (5) gives\n\n\\[\nb = -\\bigl(n-t+1-(a+b)\\bigr)\\bigl(a-(t+1)\\bigr)+(n-t+1),\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to the identity \\(b=b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\widetilde L_c\\).  Consequently **every** point of \\(U\\) lies on one of the lines \\(\\widetilde L_c\\).\n\nDistinct values of \\(c\\) yield distinct lines, so we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(U\\).\n\n#### 4.  The whole family\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\) (the \\(L_i\\) cover the part with \\(a\\le t\\), the \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) cover the remaining part \\(U\\)).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\nSince the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), the necessary co