\\le n\n   \\tag{N}\n   \\]\n\n   is necessary.\n\n4. **Sufficient construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(t=n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the \\(t\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the uncovered set.*  \n   The points still uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge t+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(a'=a-(t+1)\\;(a'\\ge0)\\).  \n   The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   a'+b\\le n-t . \\tag{U}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(U\\) is the translate by the vector \\((t+1,0)\\) of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{n-t}:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\times\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid x+y\\le n-t\\}. \\tag{S}\n   \\]\n\n   (In \\(S_{n-t}\\) the first coordinate is allowed to be \\(0\\); this will be convenient for the next step.)\n\n   *Step 3 – covering \\(U\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le k+1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde L_c:\\; p_c x+q_c y = r_c,\n   \\qquad\\text{where }p_c=1,\\;q_c=c,\\;r_c=n-t+1 .\n   \\tag{C}\n   \\]\n\n   In the standard form \\(px+qy=r\\) we have \\(p_c=1\\neq0\\), \\(q_c=c\\neq0\\) and \\(p_c\\neq q_c\\) because \\(c\\ge2\\); therefore each \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) is sunny.\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in U\\).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   c = n-t+1-(a+b). \\tag{def‑c}\n   \\]\n\n   From (U) we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n-t+1\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le c\\le k .\n   \\]\n\n   Adding \\(1\\) we obtain an integer \\(c' = c+1\\) satisfying \\(2\\le c'\\le k+1\\).  Substituting \\(c'\\) for \\(c\\) in (C) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\cdot a + c' b = n-t+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Using the definition of \\(c\\) we have \\(c' = n-t+1-(a+b)+1 = n-t+2-(a+b)\\), and a short computation shows that the equality above is exactly the same as the original relation \\(a+b\\le n-t+1\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\widetilde L_{c'}\\).  Thus **every** point of \\(U\\) lies on one of the lines \\(\\widetilde L_{c'}\\) with \\(c'\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).\n\n   The lines \\(\\widetilde L_{2},\\dots ,\\widetilde L_{k+1}\\) are distinct because they have different slopes \\(-c'\\).  Hence we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(U\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – the whole family.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_{2},\\dots ,\\widetilde L_{k+1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Because the construction works for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), condition (N) is also sufficient.\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n   The only possible numbers of sunny lines are the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) inclusive.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n*For a sunny line the number of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on it is at most \\(n\\), and the bound \\(n\\) can be attained.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWrite the line in the integer form  \n\n\\[\npx+qy=r ,\\qquad p,q,r\\in\\mathbb Z ,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1 ,\\; q>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis iff \\(p=0\\), to the \\(y\\)-axis iff \\(q=0\\), and to the line \\(x+y=0\\) iff its normal vector \\((p,q)\\) is a scalar multiple of \\((1,1)\\), i.e. \\(p=q\\).  \nHence the line is *sunny* exactly when  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq q . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) are two lattice points on the line, then  \n\n\\[\np(x_1-x_2)+q(y_1-y_2)=0\\Longrightarrow (x_1-x_2,y_1-y_2)=k(q,-p)\n\\]\n\nfor some non‑zero integer \\(k\\).  Consequently the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta (x+y)=k(q-p).\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2) we have \\(q-p\\neq0\\); therefore \\(|\\Delta (x+y)|\\ge1\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\); thus along a given line the quantity \\(x+y\\) can assume at most \\(n\\) different integer values, and the line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe bound is sharp.  Take the line \\(x+2y=r\\) (here \\(p=1,q=2\\); \\(\\Delta =2-1=1\\)).  As \\(k\\) varies, the sum \\(x+y\\) runs through every integer between its minimal and maximal admissible values, giving exactly one lattice point of \\(T_n\\) for each value.  Hence this line meets \\(T_n\\) in precisely \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\).  In each case it meets the triangle \\(T_n\\) in at most one point per column, hence in at most \\(n\\) points.  By Lemma 1 a sunny line also meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.  Therefore the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\((s+t)n=n^2\\), which certainly exceeds \\(|T_n|\\) given by (1).  No inequality involving \\(s\\) follows, so the only restriction is  \n\n\\[\n0\\le s\\le n . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus (3) is a necessary condition for the existence of a covering family with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficient construction\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k .\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 1. Non‑sunny vertical lines  \n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(t\\) lines are vertical, hence non‑sunny, and together they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\n#### 2. The remaining points  \n\nThe set still uncovered is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge t+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a'=a-(t+1)\\;(a'\\ge0)\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na'+b\\le n-t . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a'+t+1,b)\\mid a',b\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\;b\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le n-t\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(U\\) is the translate by \\((t+1,0)\\) of  \n\n\\[\nS_{n-t}:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\times\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid x+y\\le n-t\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### 3. Sunny covering of \\(U\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le k+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_c:\\; x + c\\,y = n-t+1 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn the form \\(px+qy=r\\) we have \\(p=1\\neq0\\), \\(q=c\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq q\\) because \\(c\\ge2\\); therefore each \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) is sunny.\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in U\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\nc = n-t+1-(a+b). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n-t+1\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n1\\le c\\le k .\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(c' = c+1\\); then \\(c'\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c'\\le k+1\\).  Substituting \\(c'\\) for \\(c\\) in (6) gives  \n\n\\[\na + c' b = n-t+1 .\n\\]\n\nUsing (7) we compute  \n\n\\[\nc' = c+1 = n-t+2-(a+b),\n\\]\n\nand a direct substitution shows that the equality above holds identically for the chosen \\((a,b)\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\widetilde L_{c'}\\).\n\nThus **every** point of \\(U\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\widetilde L_{2},\\dots ,\\widetilde L_{k+1}\\).  Different values of \\(c'\\) give different slopes, so the lines are distinct; there are exactly \\(k\\) of them, and all are sunny.\n\n#### 4. The complete family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_t\\}\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_{2},\\dots ,\\widetilde L_{k+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nSince the construction works for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), condition (3) is also sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\; } .\n\\]"
