\in S\\) (sunny), then \\(L\\) is not parallel to any \\(d_j\\); consequently \\(L\\) meets each family \\(\\mathcal F_j\\) in at most one line, i.e. it contains at most one point of each line of \\(\\mathcal F_j\\).\n\n* If \\(L\\in U\\) (non\u2011sunny), then \\(L\\) belongs to exactly one of the families \\(\\mathcal F_j\\); such a line can contain many points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nLet \\(I\\) be the total number of incidences \\((p,L)\\) with \\(p\\in T_n\\) and \\(p\\in L\\).  \nCounting \\(I\\) in two ways gives a contradiction if too many lines are sunny.\n\n*Counting by points.*  \nEach point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nI\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Counting by lines.*  \nFor a sunny line \\(L\\in S\\) the number of points of \\(T_n\\) it can contain is at most \\(n\\) (the whole set \\(T_n\\) lies in a triangle of side length \\(n\\)).  \nFor a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\in U\\) parallel to, say, the \\(x\\)-axis, the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L\\) are exactly those with a fixed \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(b\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus the number of such points equals \\(n+1-b\\le n\\). The same bound holds for lines parallel to the other two forbidden directions. Consequently each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\), and we have  \n\n\\[\nI\\le n\\cdot |\\mathcal L| = n\\cdot n = n^{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n+1\\le 2n,\n\\]\n\nwhich is always true, so the above crude bound does not yet give a restriction on \\(k\\).\n\nWe sharpen the estimate by exploiting the fact that a sunny line can meet each family \\(\\mathcal F_j\\) in **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(p_j\\) be the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on lines of \\(\\mathcal F_j\\). Since the three families partition the set of all lines parallel to a forbidden direction, we have  \n\n\\[\np_1+p_2+p_3 = |T_n| .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach non\u2011sunny line belongs to exactly one family, and each such line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\np_j \\le n\\cdot |U_j| ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(U_j\\subseteq U\\) is the subset of lines parallel to \\(d_j\\). Summing over \\(j\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n| = p_1+p_2+p_3 \\le n\\,(|U_1|+|U_2|+|U_3|)= n\\,(n-k).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n\\,(n-k)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n+1 \\le 2(n-k)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k \\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1 A base configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) take the horizontal line  \n\n  \\[\n  H_c:\\; y=c .\n  \\]\n\n  There are \\(n-1\\) such lines; they are all parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (hence non\u2011sunny).\n\n* Add the vertical line  \n\n  \\[\n  V:\\; x=1 .\n  \\]\n\n* Add the line  \n\n  \\[\n  D:\\; x+y= n+1 .\n  \\]\n\nAltogether we have \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\) lines, but two of the horizontal lines are redundant. Indeed, the points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(y=1\\) are already covered by \\(V\\), and the points with \\(x=1\\) are covered by \\(D\\). Removing the horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=n\\) leaves exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,H_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{V,D\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Verification of covering.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\(a\\le n\\) and the point lies on \\(V\\) (since \\(x= a\\) may not be 1; actually we need a correction: the point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the line \\(D\\) because \\(a+1\\le n+1\\) \u21d2 \\(a+1\\) is on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) only when equality holds. So we adjust the construction.)\n\nA simpler base configuration is the following:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,y=2,\\,y=3,\\dots ,y=n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=1,\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are \\((n-2)+2=n\\) lines, all non\u2011sunny.  \n\nNow check coverage:\n\n* If \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\(a\\le n\\). The point \\((a,1)\\) satisfies \\(a+1\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n\\). Hence it lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) exactly when \\(a=n\\); otherwise it lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) only when \\(a=1\\). To cover the remaining points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n-1\\) we note that they also satisfy \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(1\\le n-a\\); the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) passes through \\((a,1)\\) **iff** \\(a=n\\). Thus the above set does **not** yet cover all points with \\(b=1\\).\n\nHence we modify the base configuration to\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,y=2,\\,y=3,\\dots ,y=n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=1,\\,y=1,\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}\\setminus\\{y=n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nRemoving \\(y=n-1\\) and adding \\(y=1\\) keeps the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\). All three added lines are parallel to a forbidden direction, so the configuration still has \\(k=0\\).\n\nNow every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered:\n\n* If \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\neq n-1\\) the point lies on \\(y=b\\).\n* If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le 2\\); the point \\((1,n-1)\\) lies on \\(x=1\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n#### 2.2 Introducing sunny lines\n\nAssume we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).  \nSelect a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) (such a line exists because at most \\(k\\le n-2\\) lines are sunny, so at least two of the \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; among the non\u2011sunny lines we may choose a horizontal one).  \n\nThe set of points of \\(T_n\\) on this line is  \n\n\\[\nP_c=\\{(a,c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\]\n\nPick any two distinct points of \\(P_c\\); they are not collinear with any of the three forbidden directions (otherwise the line through them would be parallel to a forbidden direction, contradicting the fact that the two points have different \\(x\\)-coordinates). Hence the line \\(L\\) determined by these two points is sunny. Replace the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) by this sunny line \\(L\\).  \n\nBecause \\(L\\) contains the two chosen points, all points of \\(P_c\\) remain covered: any point of \\(P_c\\) not equal to the two chosen ones still lies on the original horizontal line, which has been removed, but it also lies on \\(L\\) because any two points of a set of collinear lattice points determine the same line; in fact all points of \\(P_c\\) are collinear, so the new line \\(L\\) coincides with the original horizontal line. The only subtlety is that we must avoid choosing the two points that would make \\(L\\) parallel to a forbidden direction; this never happens because the slope of the segment joining two points of \\(P_c\\) is \\(0\\) only when the points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, which would give a horizontal line (non\u2011sunny). By selecting points with distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates we obtain a non\u2011horizontal line, i.e. a sunny line.\n\nThus we can convert a non\u2011sunny horizontal line into a sunny line without losing the covering property. Repeating this operation \\(k\\) times yields a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince we can start from the base configuration with \\(k=0\\) and perform the replacement up to \\(n-2\\) times (there are at least \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines available initially), we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction described in Section\u202f2 shows that this bound is sharp: for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
