pe different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) can intersect the region\n\\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge 1,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points,\nbecause moving one unit in the \\(x\\)-direction changes the \\(y\\)-coordinate\nby a non\u2011integer amount, so the line can meet at most one point of each\nvertical strip \\(1\\le x\\le n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for vertical, horizontal and slope \\(-1\\) lines.\nHence any family of \\(m\\) lines covers at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\nSince \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}> (n-1)n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), we must have\n\\(m\\ge n\\). Consequently any admissible configuration uses exactly \\(n\\)\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\\\\n&L_n:\\;y=1,\\\\\n&L_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three families have slopes \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so none is sunny.\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence lies on\n\\(x=a\\)), or \\(a=n\\) (then \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on \\(y=1\\)), or\n\\(a+b=n+1\\) (the point lies on the last line).  Thus the conditions are\nmet with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Impossibility of \\(k=1\\)\n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) with exactly one sunny line \\(L\\)\nand \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  Partition the non\u2011sunny lines into three\nsub\u2011families:\n\n* vertical lines \\(V_j:\\;x=c_j\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(H_j:\\;y=d_j\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\) lines \\(D_j:\\;x+y=e_j\\).\n\nEach of these families can contain at most one line with a given\nintercept, otherwise the two lines would coincide.\n\nConsider the set\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nthe lattice points on the boundary \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Exactly one of these\npoints, namely \\((1,n)\\)