+y=n+1\\) is present, the remaining points are exactly \\(S_{n-1}\\). Two sunny lines can cover at most \\(2n\\) points, but \\(|S_{n-1}|=n(n-1)/2>2n\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\) (the case \\(n=3\\) is checked directly).  \n   If the diagonal line is absent, the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines can meet the diagonal in at most \\(n-2\\) points, leaving at least two diagonal points uncovered – contradiction.  \n   Therefore \\(k=2\\) is also impossible.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   All these lines are parallel to the axes, hence non‑sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (so it lies on \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n\\) (then \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on \\(y=1\\)).  \n   Thus the covering condition is fulfilled with \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Inductive construction for all \\(k\\ge3\\).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none of them is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and together they contain the six points of \\(S_3\\). Hence the statement holds for \\((n,k)=(3,3)\\).  \n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\) for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and some \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   From \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\) we obtain configurations for \\((n+1,k)\\) and \\((n+1,k+1)\\) as follows.\n\n   - **From \\((n,k)\\) to \\((n+1,k)\\).**  \n     Because \\(k\\le n-1\\) we may assume that \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\) contains at least one non‑sunny line (otherwise \\(k=n\\) and the case \\((n+1,k)\\) is obtained from the next 