nAdd the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=n+1,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(V\\) covers the new point \\((n+1,1)\\) and \\(D\\) covers the new point \\((1,n+1)\\).  \nNow delete **any** non‑sunny line of the original configuration (possible because at least one exists).  \nThe total number of lines is \\((n+1)\\); the number of sunny lines is unchanged (\\(k\\)).  \nAll old points remain covered: a deleted vertical line \\(x=c\\) can be removed only if the column \\(c\\) contains a point already covered by a sunny line (this can be arranged during the construction of \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\); for the base case it is true, and the inductive step preserves the property).  Hence we obtain a valid configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2.2 From \\((n,k)\\) to \\((n+1,k+1)\\)\n\nAgain \\(k\\le n-1\\) guarantees the existence of a non‑sunny line in \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\).  \n\nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=n+1\n\\]\n\n(which covers the new point \\((n+1,1)\\)).  \nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=-n\\,x+(n+1)n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) passes through the two new points \\((n+1,1)\\) and \\((1,n+1)\\); its slope \\(-n\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nNow delete any non‑sunny line of the original configuration.  \nThe resulting family has \\(n+1\\) lines, exactly \\(k+1\\) of which are sunny (the newly added line \\(L\\) is sunny and we removed a non‑sunny one).  All points are covered: the new vertical line and the new sunny line take care of the two new points, while the remaining points are covered as before.  \n\nThus a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2.3 Reaching all pairs \\((n,k)\\)\n\nStarting from the base configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{3,3}\\) we can increase \\(n\\) while keeping \\(k\\) unchanged by repeatedly applying the construction of §4.2.1, and we can increase both \\(n\\) and \\(k\\) by one by applying the construction of §4.2.2.  By a suitable sequence of these two operations we obtain a configuration for **every** pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\n- \\(k=0\\) is realizable (Section 3).  \n- \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\) are impossible (Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2).  \n- Every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable (Section 4).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ 3\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]"
