Any line that contains two points of \\(D_n\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is non\u2011sunny.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(D_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1  (\\(k=1\\) is impossible)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly one sunny line \\(L\\).  \nIf the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) is used among the non\u2011sunny lines, then all points of \\(D_n\\) are already covered and the remaining points are the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n\\setminus D_n=S_{\\,n-1}\n\\]\n\n(which consists of the points with \\(a+b\\le n\\)).  \nThe single sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points, whereas  \n\n\\[\n|S_{\\,n-1}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2>n\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\nThus the covering is impossible.\n\nIf the diagonal line is **not** used, then each of the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines can meet \\(D_n\\) in at most one point, so at most \\(n-1\\) diagonal points are covered by non\u2011sunny lines.  The unique sunny line can cover at most one further diagonal point, leaving at least one point of \\(D_n\\) uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  (\\(k=2\\) is impossible)\n\nAssume exactly two sunny lines.  As before each sunny line meets \\(D_n\\) in at most one point, so at most two diagonal points are covered by sunny lines.\n\n*If the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) is present*, all diagonal points are covered and the remaining points form \\(S_{\\,n-1}\\).  Two sunny lines together contain at most \\(2n\\) points, while  \n\n\\[\n|S_{\\,n-1}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2>2n\\qquad (n\\ge4).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) one checks directly that no two sunny lines can cover the three points of \\(S_3\\setminus D_3\\).  Hence this case is impossible.\n\n*If the diagonal line is absent*, the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines can meet the diagonal in at most \\(n-2\\) points, leaving at least two diagonal points uncovered \u2013 again impossible. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  All these lines are parallel to the axes, therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (so the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n\\); in the latter case \\(b\\le1\\) and the point is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(y=1\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  The configuration uses exactly \\(n\\) lines and \\(k=0\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Inductive construction for all \\(k\\ge3\\)\n\nWe prove that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1 Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nTheir integer points are  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),\\qquad\nL_2:\\;(1,2),(3,1),\\qquad\nL_3:\\;(1,3),(2,1),\n\\]\n\nwhich together are exactly the six points of \\(S_3\\).  Thus a configuration with \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2 Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and some \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\) exists.  \nWe show how to obtain configurations for \\((n+1,k)\\) and \\((n+1,k+1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2.1 From \\((n,k)\\) to \\((n+1,k)\\)\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-1\\) we may assume that \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\