\) is always attainable.\n\n   *Construction\u202f\\(n-2\\) (exactly two non\u2011sunny lines).*  \n   Use the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   and for each \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\) the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; \\text{through }(1,i)\\text{ and }(i,n+1-i).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1-2i}{\\,i-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \n   The family \\(\\{y=1,\\;x+y=n+1,\\;\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and covers all points of \\(T_n\\). Therefore \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n   *Construction\u202f\\(n-1\\) (exactly one non\u2011sunny line).*  \n   Take the non\u2011sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Choose a **derangement** \\(\\sigma\\) of the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (i.e. a permutation with \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\) for every \\(i\\)).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\;\\text{through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,\\sigma(i)).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\) the slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{\\sigma(i)-1}{i-1}\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover all points of \\(B\\setminus\\{(1,1)\\}\\) and all points of \\(L\\setminus\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  \n   The remaining point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by the sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n   Altogether we have \\(n\\) lines, exactly one of which (\\(H\\)) is non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *Construction for intermediate values.*  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (or \\(k\\le n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)) we start from the construction for \\(k=n-2\\) and replace \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) by non\u2011sunny lines that still cover the points they previously c