\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non‑existence for \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing at most one non‑sunny line.  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\nConsider the convex hull \\(\\Delta\\) of \\(S_n\\); it is the right‑angled triangle with vertices  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,1),\\qquad B=(n,1),\\qquad C=(1,n).\n\\]\n\nThe three sides of \\(\\Delta\\) are respectively parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis and the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nA sunny line cannot be parallel to any side of \\(\\Delta\\); therefore a sunny line can intersect \\(\\Delta\\) in at most one of its sides.\n\nLet \\(m\\) be the number of sunny lines; by assumption \\(m\\ge n-1\\).  \nEach sunny line can contain points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on at most one side of \\(\\Delta\\).  \nHence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by all sunny lines is at most the total number of lattice points that lie on the three sides of \\(\\Delta\\).\n\nThe lattice points on the three sides are:\n\n* on \\(AB\\): \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) – \\(n\\) points;\n* on \\(AC\\): \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) – \\(n\\) points;\n* on \\(BC\\): \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1,\\;a,b\\ge 1\\) – \\(n-1\\) points.\n\nThus at most \\(3n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on the sides.  \nAll interior points of \\(S_n\\) (those with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\)) are not on any side.  \nThe number of interior points equals  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-(3n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-(3n-1)=\\frac{n^2-7n+2}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 5\\) the quantity in (2) is positive; consequently there exist interior points that must be covered by sunny lines.  \nBut a sunny line that is not parallel to any side cannot contain two distinct interior points of \\(\\Delta\\) (otherwise the line would be fo