            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminaries\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) form the integer lattice points of the right‑angled triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\operatorname{conv}\\{(1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three forbidden directions are exactly the directions of the three sides of \\(\\Delta\\):\nhorizontal \\((1,0)\\), vertical \\((0,1)\\) and slope \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of these three sides; otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A bound for a sunny line\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }L\\text{ is sunny, then }|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Write the line in the integer form \\(ax+by=c\\) with coprime integers\n\\(a,b\\) and \\(ab\\neq0\\) (otherwise the line would be horizontal or vertical).\nThe slope of \\(L\\) equals \\(-a/b\\); the condition “not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)’’\nmeans \\(a\\neq b\\).\n\nAll integer points of \\(L\\) are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+tb,\\;y_0-ta),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is a particular integer solution of \\(ax+by=c\\).\nSince \\(|a|\\) and \\(|b|\\) are coprime and \\(a\\neq b\\), we have\n\\(|a|+|b|\\ge3\\); consequently \\(|b|\\ge1\\) and \\(|a|\\ge2\\) or vice‑versa.\nThus when we move from one lattice point of \\(L\\) to the next, at least one\ncoordinate changes by \\(\\ge2\\).\n\nInside the triangle \\(\\Delta\\) the \\(x\\)–coordinate ranges over at most \\(n\\) integer\nvalues and the same holds for the \\(y\\)–coordinate.  Because one of the\ncoordinates changes by at least \\(2\\) each step, at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(L\\) can lie in \\(\\Delta\\),\nand therefore in \\(S_n\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  At least two non‑sunny lines for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }n\\ge5\\text{ and }S_n\\text{ is covered by }n\\text{ lines, then at least two\nof the lines are non‑sunny.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose, to the contrary, that exactly one line \\(L_0\\) is\nnon‑sunny and the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the whole side of\n\\(\\Delta\\) that is parallel to it).  By Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence the total\nnumber of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) one checks directly that  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2 =|S_n|\n\\]\n\n(the left‑hand side equals \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd and equals\n\\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) when \\(n\\) is even; in the even case the equality would\nrequire that every sunny line contain exactly \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) points, which is\nimpossible because the three points \\((2,2),(3,2),(2,3)\\) cannot all lie on a\nsingle sunny line).  Thus (3) is strictly smaller than \\(|S_n|\\), a\ncontradiction.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A covering with the maximal number of sunny lines (\\(k=n-2\\))\n\nAssume \\(n\\ge5\\).  Define the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBoth lines belong to the forbidden directions, so they are non‑sunny.\nAll points of the bottom side \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) are covered by\n\\(L_h\\); all points of the left side \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) are covered\nby \\(L_v\\).\n\nThe remaining points are the **interior points**\n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTheir cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=|S_n|-2n+1=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWe now introduce the family of \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell_d:\\;y=x+d\\qquad\\bigl(d=0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-3}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr),\\\\[1mm]\n&m_e:\\;y=-2x+2(e+1)\\qquad\\bigl(e=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil\\tfrac{n-3}{2}\\rceil\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\); neither equals \\(0,\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), so all\nlines in (7) are sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge a\\)*, set \\(d=b-a\\).  Because \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n0\\le d\\le\\frac{n-3}{2},\n\\]\n\nhence \\(d\\) belongs to the index set of the lines \\(\\ell_d\\).  Moreover\n\\(b=a+d\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_d\\).\n\n*If \\(b<a\\)*, set \\(e=a-b\\).  The same inequalities give  \n\n\\[\n1\\le e\\le\\frac{n-3}{2},\n\\]\n\nso \\(e\\) belongs to the index set of the lines \\(m_e\\).  From \\(b=a-e\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb=-2a+2(e+1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(m_e\\).\n\nThus every interior point belongs to exactly one of the lines in (7); the two\nnon‑sunny lines (4) cover the whole bottom and left sides, and consequently the\n\\(n\\) lines\n\n\\[\nL_h,\\;L_v,\\;\\ell_0,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\lfloor\\frac{n-3}{2}\\rfloor},\n\\;m_1,\\dots ,m_{\\lceil\\frac{n-3}{2}\\rceil}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ncover all of \\(S_n\\).  The number of sunny lines in (8) is precisely\n\\(n-2\\).  Hence for every \\(n\\ge5\\) a covering with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Obtaining all smaller values of \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from the configuration (8) (which has \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines) we can\nreduce the number of sunny lines arbitrarily.\n\nChoose any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\).  Replace \\(t\\) distinct sunny\nlines of (8) by \\(t\\) distinct lines that are parallel to a side of \\(\\Delta\\)\nbut lie **outside** the triangle (for instance the vertical lines\n\\(x=n,n+1,\\dots\\)).  Such a line is non‑sunny and contains no point of\n\\(S_n\\); therefore the covering property is unchanged while the number of\nsunny lines is reduced by \\(t\\).  Taking \\(t=n-2-k\\) we obtain a covering with\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The small values \\(n=3,4\\)\n\n*Case \\(n=3\\).*  \n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) (six points).  \n\n*   \\(k=0\\):  the three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=1\\) cover all points.  \n*   \\(k=1\\):  the lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) (non‑sunny) together with the sunny line\n    \\(y=x\\) (through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\)) cover the whole set.  \n\nThe three points \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) cannot be covered by only two sunny\nlines, because any line through two of them has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)\n(forbidden).  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\{k\\mid\\text{a covering exists}\\}=\\{0,1\\}\\qquad (n=3).\n\\]\n\n*Case \\(n=4\\).*  The set \\(S_4\\) consists of fifteen points.  The following\nfamilies of four lines realise the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\nk=0:& y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3;\\\\[1mm]\nk=1:& y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=5,\\;y=x\\;( \\text{slope }1);\\\\[1mm]\nk=3:& y=1,\\;x+y=5,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=-2x+6 .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIn each family all fifteen points of \\(S_4\\) are covered.  As in the case\n\\(n=3\\), the three points \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) cannot be covered by only two\nsunny lines, so \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\{k\\mid\\text{a covering exists}\\}=\\{0,1,3\\}\\qquad (n=4).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nCombining Lemma 2, the construction of Section 4 and the reductions of\nSection 5, together with the explicit analysis of the small cases, we obtain\nthe complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
