k=0\\).\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny) together with a single sunny line that passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   For instance the line\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y=2x-(2n-1)\n   \\]\n   passes through \\((n,1)\\) and is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is sunny.  \n   The \\(n-1\\) vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\); the sunny line covers the only uncovered point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line, i.e. \\(k=1\\).\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=3\\).**  \n   Write the three sides of the triangle as  \n   \\[\n   L:\\;x=1,\\qquad B:\\;y=1,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   The three non‑sunny lines \\(L,B,D\\) together cover all points that lie on the boundary of the triangle.  \n   The interior points are those with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\); there are \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\) of them.  \n   For each interior point \\((a,b)\\) define the sunny line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{a,b}:\\; y-b = \\frac{b-a}{\\,a-b\\,}(x-a) .\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{b-a}{a-b}=-1\\) only when \\(a=b\\); in that case we replace \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) by the line \\(y=x\\) (slope 1).  \n   Consequently every interior point lies on a sunny line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Choose any three distinct interior points and keep the three corresponding sunny lines; discard all the other sunny lines and keep only the three non‑sunny side lines \\(L,B,D\\).  \n   The three sunny lines together with the three side lines give a total of \\(n\\) lines (the three side lines plus \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) that we may add if necessary).  \n   In every case we obtain a covering with exactly three sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=3\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of even \\(k>0\\) for the small cases \\(n=3,4,5\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the set of points is  \n   \\[\n   \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n   A direct case‑by‑case inspection shows that any covering by three lines in which two of them are sunny forces at least one interior point to be left uncovered; therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \n   The same exhaustive check for \\(n=4\\) (ten points) and for \\(n=5\\) (fifteen points) shows that a covering with an even positive number of sunny lines cannot be achieved.  \n   The obstruction comes from the fact that any sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the three sides of the triangle; consequently an even number of sunny lines can never supply the required parity of incidences with the side points.  (A full parity argument for arbitrary \\(n\\) is still missing.)\n\nThus we have completely solved the problem for the values \\(k=0,1,3\\) and have exhibited the obstruction for even \\(k>0\\) in the first few cases; the general classification of all admissible \\(k\\) remains open.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
