\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and the impossibility results for \\(k=1,2\\) with the inductive construction for all \\(k\\ge3\\) yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other lines are called **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be any line.  Write it in the form \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\).  \nIf \\(m\\) is finite, write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms (\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0\\)).  \nThe integer points of \\(L\\) are exactly the points \\((x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\) for some particular solution \\((x_0,y_0)\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) the coordinate \\(a\\) can take at most the values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lie on \\(L\\).  The same bound holds for vertical, horizontal and slope \\(-1\\) lines.\n\nConsequently any family of \\(m\\) lines covers at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Since \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2> (n-1)n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), at least \\(n\\) lines are necessary.  In the sequel we always use exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The cases \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\) are impossible\n\nDenote the diagonal set  \n\n\\[\nD_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line that contains two points of \\(D_n\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is