 line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Q\\).  \nIf \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms, the integer points on the line are\n\\((x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) the coordinate \\(a\\) can take at most the values \\(1,\\dots ,n\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lie on the line.  The same bound is obvious for vertical, horizontal and slope \\(-1\\) lines. ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently any family of \\(m\\) lines covers at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(S_n\\); because \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2> (n-1)n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), at least \\(n\\) lines are necessary.  In what follows we always use exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  (Impossibility of \\(k=1\\))\n\nA sunny line can contain at most one point of the diagonal \\(D_n\\) (any line through two diagonal points has slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n*If the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) is present*, all points of \\(D_n\\) are already covered.  The remaining points are \\(S_{n-1}\\).  The unique sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points, whereas \\(|S_{n-1}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\); contradiction.\n\n*If the diagonal line is absent*, the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines can meet \\(D_n\\) in at most \\(n-1\\) points, leaving at least one diagonal point uncovered because the sunny line can cover at most one diagonal point.  Contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  (Impossibility of \\(k=2\\))\n\nAgain each sunny line meets \\(D_n\\) in at most one point, so at most two diagonal points can be covered by sunny lines.\n\n*Case 1: the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) is used.*  \nAll diagonal points are covered; the uncovered set is \\(S_{n-1}\\).  \n\nFor \\(n=3,4,5\\) a direct inspection shows that two sunny lines cannot cover \\(S_{n-1}\\) (the numbers of points are \\(3,6,10\\) respectively, while two sunny lines together contain at most \\(2n\\le10\\) points and a simple check of all possible slopes shows no covering).  \n\nFor \\(n\\ge6\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_{n-1}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2>2n,\n\\]\n\nwhereas two sunny lines together contain at most \\(2n\\) points by Lemma 1.  Hence covering is impossible.\n\n*Case 2: the diagonal line is not used.*  \nThe \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines can meet the diagonal in at most \\(n-2\\) points, leaving at least two diagonal points uncovered because the two sunny lines together can cover at most two diagonal points.  Contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the axes, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (so the point lies on \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n\\) (then \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on \\(y=1\\)).  Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  The configuration uses \\(n\\) lines and \\(k=0\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nLet \\(r=n-k\\) (so \\(0\\le r\\le n-3\\)).  \n\n**Non‑sunny lines.**  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,r\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i+1 .\n\\]\n\nAdd the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(r+1\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;y=-\\frac{j}{j+1}\\,x+(j+2).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.  \nA short computation shows that \\(L_j\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\n(1,j+1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(j+2,1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(k-1\\) sunny lines together cover the set  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(1,2),(1,3),\\dots ,(1,k)\\}\\cup\\{(3,1),(4,1),\\dots ,(k+1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le r+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a-1}\\) (for \\(a=1\\) we use the horizontal line \\(H\\)).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge r+2\\)*, then using \\(r=n-k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(r+2)=k-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(2\\le b\\le k-1\\) (the case \\(b=1\\) is already covered by \\(H\\)).  \nSet \\(j=b-1\\) (so \\(1\\le j\\le k-2\\)).  \nFrom (4) the line \\(L_j\\) contains the point \\((j+2,1)\\) and has slope \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\).  \nWriting the equation of \\(L_j\\) as in (3) and substituting \\(y=b\\) gives\n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{(j+2)-b}{1+\\frac{j}{j+1}}=j+2-(b-1)=a .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{b-1}\\).\n\nAll possibilities have been exhausted, so every element of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(r+1)+(k-1)=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k-1\\) of them are sunny; adding the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which is non‑sunny) when \\(r=0\\) does not change the count of sunny lines.  Hence the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\n* \\(k=0\\) is realizable (construction above).  \n* \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\) are impossible (Lemmas 2 and 3).  \n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists (explicit construction).  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ 3\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]"
