ontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   The single sunny line can meet \\(D_n\\) in at most one point, so at most \\(n\\) diagonal points are covered in total.  \n   Since \\(|D_n|=n\\), **all** diagonal points must be covered, which forces the sunny line to pass through a diagonal point.  \n   Removing that diagonal point, the remaining \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines have to cover the set \\(S_n\\setminus D_n\\).  \n   Every non\u2011sunny line is parallel to an axis or has slope \\(-1\\); such a line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\setminus D_n\\).  \n   Thus the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)(n-1)=n^2-2n+1<|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n   \\]\n\n   again a contradiction.  \n   Hence **\\(k=1\\) is impossible** for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   The same two\u2011case analysis works, now using the fact that the two sunny lines together can meet the diagonal in at most two points.\n\n   *Case\u202fA \u2013 the diagonal line is present.*  \n   The remaining points are \\(S_{n-1}\\).  \n   Each sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n-1}\\); the \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines together contain at most \\((n-3)(n-1)\\) points.  \n   Hence the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2n+(n-3)(n-1)=n^2-3n+2<\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.\n\n   *Case\u202fB \u2013 the diagonal line is absent.*  \n   The two sunny lines can meet the diagonal in at most two points, so the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines must meet the diagonal in the remaining \\(n-2\\) points; this forces each of them to be vertical or horizontal, and each such line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\setminus D_n\\).  \n   Consequently the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2n+(n-2)(n-1)=n^2-2n+2<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  \n   Therefore **\\(k=2\\) is also impossible** for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   All these lines are parallel to the axes, hence non\u2011sunny, and they clearly cover every point of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The configuration uses exactly \\(n\\) lines and contains no sunny line.\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n   Write \\(r=n-k\\) (so \\(0\\le r\\le n-3\\)).  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   - The \\(r\\) vertical lines \\(V_i:\\;x=i+1\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,r\\).  \n   - The horizontal line \\(H:\\;y=1\\).  \n\n   These are \\(r+1\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For \\(j=1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\;y=-\\frac{j}{j+1}\\,x+(j+2).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.  \n   A direct substitution shows that  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j\\text{ passes through }(1,j+1)\\text{ and }(j+2,1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the \\(k-1\\) sunny lines together cover the points  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(1,2),(1,3),\\dots ,(1,k)\\}\\cup\\{(3,1),(4,1),\\dots ,(k+1,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification that all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   - **If \\(a\\le r+1\\)**, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a-1}\\) (for \\(a=1\\) we use the horizontal line \\(H\\)).  \n\n   - **If \\(a\\ge r+2\\)**, then using \\(r=n-k\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(r+2)=k-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Thus \\(2\\le b\\le k-1\\) (the case \\(b=1\\) is already covered by \\(H\\)).  \n     Set \\(j=b-1\\) (so \\(1\\le j\\le k-2\\)).  \n     From (6) the line \\(L_j\\) contains the point \\((j+2,1)\\) and has slope \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\).  \n     Solving the equation of \\(L_j\\) for \\(x\\) with the given ordinate \\(y=b\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     x=\\frac{(j+2)-b}{1+\\frac{j}{j+1}}=j+2-(b-