            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and supplied a fully rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ 3\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\qquad\\text{(the set of points that must be covered).}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (maximal number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a line).**  \n   Any line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   *Proof.*  If the line is vertical, the points have the same abscissa \\(x\\); the condition\n   \\(x+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le n+1-x\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) possibilities.  \n   The same argument works for a horizontal line.  \n   If the line has slope \\(-1\\) it is of the form \\(x+y=c\\); the inequality\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(c\\le n+1\\), and the integer solutions of \\(a+b=c\\) are\n   \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), again at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\).**  \n   We distinguish two exhaustive cases.\n\n   *Case A – the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (the “diagonal’’) belongs to the family.*  \n   Removing this line leaves the set  \n   \\[\n   S_{n-1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n   which contains \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) points.  \n   By Lemma 1 each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines can contain at most \\(n\\) points of\n   \\(S_{n-1}\\); consequently the total number of points that can be covered is at most\n   \\((n-1)n=n^2-n\\).  Since\n   \\[\n   n^2-n-\\frac{n(n-1)}2=\\frac{n(n-1)}2>0\\qquad(n\\ge 3),\n   \\]\n   the points of \\(S_{n-1}\\) cannot all be covered.  Hence a configuration containing the\n   diagonal line is impossible for any \\(k\\).\n\n   *Case B – the diagonal line is absent.*  \n   All \\(n\\) lines are either vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Such a line can meet the diagonal set\n   \\[\n   D_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n   in at most one point (two points of \\(D_n\\) determine a line of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Therefore at most \\(n\\) points of \\(D_n\\) can be covered, i.e. **every** point of\n   \\(D_n\\) must be covered.  Consequently each of the \\(n\\) lines meets \\(D_n\\) in exactly\n   one point, which forces every line to be either vertical or horizontal (a line of\n   slope \\(-1\\) would meet \\(D_n\\) in two points).  Hence all \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny,\n   contradicting the assumption that \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=2\\).  \n\n   The two cases together show that **\\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\) are impossible** for every\n   \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) together with the horizontal line\n   \\(y=1\\).  All these lines are parallel to the axes, hence non‑sunny, and they clearly\n   cover every point of \\(S_n\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n   Write \\(r=n-k\\) (so \\(0\\le r\\le n-3\\)).  \n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i+1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,r),\\qquad H:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n   These are \\(r+1\\) distinct lines, all parallel to the axes.\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For \\(j=1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\;y=-\\frac{j}{j+1}\\,x+(j+2). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each\n   \\(L_j\\) is sunny.  A direct substitution shows that\n   \\[\n   L_j\\text{ passes through }(1,j+1)\\text{ and }(j+2,1). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed\n   lines.*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   - **If \\(a\\le r+1\\)**, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a-1}\\) (for\n     \\(a=1\\) we use the horizontal line \\(H\\)).\n\n   - **If \\(a\\ge r+2\\)**, then using \\(r=n-k\\) we have\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(r+2)=k-1 .\n     \\]\n     Hence \\(2\\le b\\le k-1\\) (the case \\(b=1\\) is already covered by \\(H\\)).\n     Put \\(j=b-1\\) (so \\(1\\le j\\le k-2\\)).  \n     By (6) the line \\(L_j\\) contains the two points \\((1,b)\\) and \\((j+2,1)\\).\n     Because \\(L_j\\) is a straight line, any point with ordinate \\(b\\) on \\(L_j\\) has\n     abscissa\n     \\[\n     x = (j+2)-\\frac{b-1}{\\,j/(j+1)} = (j+2)-(b-1)=a .\n     \\]\n     (the last equality follows from the definition \\(j=b-1\\)).  Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n     \\(L_j\\).\n\n   All possibilities have been exhausted, so every element of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n   The total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   (r+1)+(k-1)=n,\n   \\]\n   and exactly \\(k-1\\) of them are sunny; adding the horizontal line \\(H\\) (which is\n   non‑sunny) gives a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   - \\(k=0\\) is realizable (step 4).  \n   - \\(k=1,2\\) are impossible (step 3).  \n   - Every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable (step 5).  \n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ 3\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summa