emaining\n   \\(n-1\\) lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n-1}\\); consequently at most\n   \\((n-1)n=n^2-n\\) points can be covered.  Since  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}2 > n^2-n\\qquad (n\\ge 3)\n   \\]\n\n   is false, the points of \\(S_{n-1}\\) cannot all be covered \u2013 a contradiction.\n   Hence the diagonal line is absent.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.2 \u2013 the set \\(D_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) (the \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019 points)\n   must be covered by non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Any line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with the diagonal line, which we have just\n   excluded; therefore a line of slope \\(-1\\) can meet \\(D_n\\) in at most one point.\n   A vertical line meets \\(D_n\\) in at most one point, and the same holds for a\n   horizontal line.  Consequently each point of \\(D_n\\) must be covered by a line\n   whose direction is **not** sunny, i.e. by a vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)\n   line.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.3 \u2013 counting sunny lines.*  \n   The family contains exactly \\(k\\in\\{1,2\\}\\) sunny lines, all of which have a slope\n   different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Such a sunny line can intersect \\(D_n\\) in at\n   most one point (otherwise two points of \\(D_n\\) would determine a line of slope\n   \\(-1\\)).  Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of\n   \\(D_n\\).  The remaining \\(n-k\\) points of \\(D_n\\) must be covered by the\n   \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, forcing each of those non\u2011sunny lines to meet \\(D_n\\) in\n   exactly one point.  In particular **every** non\u2011sunny line is either vertical or\n   horizontal (a line of slope \\(-1\\) would meet two points of \\(D_n\\)).  Thus all\n   non\u2011sunny lines are parallel to the axes.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.4 \u2013 contradiction.*  \n   The \\(n-k\\) axis\u2011parallel lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of the whole set\n   \\(S_n\\) (Lemma\u202f1), while the \\(k\\) sunny lines each contain at most \\(n\\) points,\n   giving a total capacity of at most \\(n\\!+\\!k\\!<\\!|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for\n   \\(k=1,2\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Hence the covering condition cannot be satisfied.\n   Therefore **\\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\) are impossible** for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\]\n\n   (there are \\(n-1\\) of them) together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n   All these lines are parallel to the axes, hence non\u2011sunny, and they clearly\n   contain every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) (the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) guarantees\n   \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b=1\\) or \\(a\\ge 2\\)).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n   Write \\(r=n-k\\) (so \\(0\\le r\\le n-3\\)).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i+1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,r),\\qquad H:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n   These are \\(r+1\\) distinct axis\u2011parallel lines.\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,(x-1)+t .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{t-1}{t}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently each\n   \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  A direct substitution shows that  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t\\text{ passes through }(1,t)\\text{ and }(t+1,1). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_r, H\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\((r+1)+(k-1)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny\n   (the \\(k-1\\) lines \\(L_t\\) together with the fact that \\(k\\ge 3\\) guarantees at\n   least one sunny line; the horizontal line \\(H\\) is non\u2011sunny).\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   - **Case 1: \\(a\\le r+1\\).**  \n     If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\); otherwise it lies on the\n     vertical line \\(V_{a-1}\\).\n\n   - **Case 2: \\(a\\ge r+2\\).**  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(r=n-k\\),\n\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(r+2)=k-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(2\\le b\\le k-1\\) (the sub\u2011case \\(b=1\\) is already covered by \\(H\\)).\n     Set \\(t=b\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le k-1\\)).  By (6) the line \\(L_t\\) contains the two\n     points \\((1,t)\\) and \\((t+1,1)\\).  Since \\((a,b)\\) has ordinate \\(b=t\\), the\n     equation of \\(L_t\\) gives\n\n     \\[\n     y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}(x-1)+t \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n     x = (t+1)-\\frac{t}{t-1}(y-t).\n     \\]\n\n     Substituting \\(y=t\\) yields \\(x=t+1\\).  Because \\(a\\ge r+2=n-k+2\\) and\n     \\(t=b\\le k-1\\), we have \\(a = (t+1)+(a-t-1)\\) with the second term equal to\n     \\(a-(t+1)\\ge 0\\).  Directly from (5) with \\(y=t\\) we obtain\n\n     \\[\n     x = (t+1)-\\frac{t}{t-1}(t-t)=t+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(a=t+1\\), i.e. \\((a,b)=(t+1,t)\\), which indeed lies on \\(L_t\\).  More\n     simply, because \\(L_t\\) passes through \\((t+1,1)\\) and has slope\n     \\(-\\dfrac{t-1}{t}\\), the unique point of \\(L_t\\) with ordinate \\(t\\) is\n     \\((t+1,t)\\); the inequality \\(a\\ge r+2\\) forces \\(a=t+1\\).  Therefore\n     \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_t\\).\n\n   All possibilities are exhausted, so every element of \\(S_n\\) is covered by a\n   line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, completing the existence part\n   for all \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   - \\(k=0\\) is realizable (Step\u202f4).  \n   - \\(k=1,2\\) are impossible (Step\u202f3).  \n   - Every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable (Step\u202f5).  \n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ 3\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n   \\]"
