(a_{i+1}-a_i)+(b_{i+1}-b_i)\n                \\ge a_i+b_i+1 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(a_i+b_i\\ge 2+(i-1)=i+1\\).  Because each point of \\(S_n\\) satisfies\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we obtain \\(i+1\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(i\\le n\\).  Thus the line\n   contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n3. **Impossibility of any restriction on \\(k\\).**  \n   The previous erroneous argument that excluded \\(k=1,2\\) relied on the false\n   claim that the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) cannot belong to the family.  In fact\n   the diagonal line is perfectly admissible (it is not sunny, its slope is\n   \\(-1\\)).  Consequently there is **no obstruction** on the number of sunny\n   lines; any value \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) can be realised.  The remainder\n   of the solution is therefore devoted to explicit constructions.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,x=3,\\dots ,x=n\\) together with the\n   horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  All these lines are parallel to the axes, hence\n   non‑sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies either \\(a=1\\) (so it\n   lies on \\(y=1\\)) or \\(a\\ge 2\\) (so it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)).\n   Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **General construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n   Write \\(r=n-k\\) (so \\(0\\le r\\le n-1\\)).  We shall use\n\n   * **\\(r\\) vertical lines**  \n     \\[\n     V_i:\\;x=i+1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,r),\n     \\]\n   * **one horizontal line**  \n     \\[\n     H:\\;y=1,\n     \\]\n   * **\\(k-1\\) sunny lines** (for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k\\))\n     \\[\n     L_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,(x-1)+t .\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{t-1}{t}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each\n   \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  A direct substitution shows that\n\n   \\[\n   L_t\\text{ passes through }(1,t)\\text{ and }(t+1,1). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_r, H\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\((r+1)+(k-1)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny\n   (the \\(k-1\\) lines \\(L_t\\) together with the fact that \\(k\\ge 1\\); when \\(k=1\\)\n   the set \\(\\{L_t\\}\\) is empty and the only line is the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\),\n   see below).\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   - **Case 1: \\(a\\le r+1\\).**  \n     If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\); otherwise it lies on\n     the vertical line \\(V_{a-1}\\).\n\n   - **Case 2: \\(a\\ge r+2\\).**  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(r=n-k\\),\n\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(r+2)=k-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(2\\le b\\le k-1\\) (the sub‑case \\(b=1\\) is already covered by \\(H\\)).\n     Set \\(t=b\\).  By (6) the line \\(L_t\\) contains the two points\n     \\((1,t)\\) and \\((t+1,1)\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{t-1}{t}\\), so the unique\n     point of \\(L_t\\) with ordinate \\(t\\) is \\((t+1,t)\\).  Since \\(a\\ge r+2=n-k+2\\)\n     and \\(t\\le k-1\\), we have \\(a=t+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)=(t+1,t)\\) lies on\n     \\(L_t\\).\n\n   All possibilities are exhausted, so every element of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n   *Special sub‑case \\(k=1\\).*  \n   Here \\(r=n-1\\).  We take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) and the\n   diagonal line \\(D:\\;x+y=n+1\\).  The diagonal is not sunny, the vertical lines\n   are not sunny, and together they cover \\(S_n\\) (the points with \\(a=1\\) lie on\n   \\(D\\), the others on the appropriate vertical line).  Thus \\(k=1\\) is also\n   realised.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   - \\(k=0\\) is realised by the construction of Step 4.  \n   - For every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) the construction of Step 5 (with the\n     special handling of \\(k=1\\)) provides a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n     covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Hence **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is admissible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
