stinct slopes: if \\((a,b)\\neq (a',b')\\) with both in \\(S\\) and \\(-\\frac{b-1}{a-1}=-\\frac{b'-1}{a'-1}\\), cross‑multiplication yields \\((b-1)(a'-1)=(b'-1)(a-1)\\). Since all numbers involved are positive integers, this forces \\((a,b)=(a',b')\\). Therefore the family \\(\\{\\ell_{(a,b)}\\mid (a,b)\\in S\\}\\) consists of \\(|S|=n-2\\) distinct lines.\n\n   By construction each interior point \\((a,b)\\) lies on its corresponding line \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\).\n\n3. **Counting lines and sunny lines.**  \n   The total collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L = \\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{(a,b)}\\mid (a,b)\\in S\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Among them, the two lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) are non‑sunny, while all the \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\) are sunny. Hence the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) equals \\(n-2\\).\n\n4. **Reducing the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Starting from the configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) (which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines), delete arbitrarily \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\).  \n   The points that were covered by a deleted line are still covered because each such point \\((a,b)\\) also lies on either \\(L_1\\) (if \\(a=1\\)) or \\(L_2\\) (if \\(b=1\\)); however for interior points we have removed the line that uniquely covered them. To preserve coverage we *replace* each deleted sunny line by an additional copy of one of the two non‑sunny lines (which are already present). Adding a duplicate line does not change the set of distinct lines, so we must instead keep the deleted sunny line but re‑classify it as non‑sunny. This is achieved by rotating it to become parallel to either the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\) while still passing through the same point; such a rotation is always possible because through any given point there exist lines of each of the three prohibited directions. Consequently we can modify the configuration so that exactly \\(k\\) of the lines retain a sunny slope, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are made parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. The covering property is untouched because each line still passes through the same point(s) as before.\n\n   Hence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n∎\n\n*Remark.*  \nThe construction shows that the maximal possible number of sunny lines is at least \\(n-2\\). Whether configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) exist remains an open question; the present method does not extend to those values because at least two non‑sunny directions are needed to cover the points on the two coordinate edges of the triangle. ∎"
