"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the following:\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*  \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-3\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=5,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=7,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-3\\,\\}, & n\\ge 9 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for even \\(n\\) every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-3\\) occurs, and for odd \\(n\\) the same holds for all \\(n\\ge9\\); the three smallest odd values of \\(n\\) are exceptional and are listed explicitly above.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Necessary upper bounds.**  \n   \u2013 Every covering must contain the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); otherwise the points \\((1,b)\\) or \\((a,1)\\) could not all be covered (they would require a distinct line for each point). Hence at least two non\u2011sunny lines are always present.  \n\n   \u2013 Let \\(N=n-2\\) be the number of interior lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n     Set  \n     \\[\n     M:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{N}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n     \\]\n     A sunny line can contain at most \\(M\\) interior points (the maximal case is a line of slope \\(1\\)).  \n\n   \u2013 If exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines are used, the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. Two of them are forced (\\(x=1,\\;y=1\\)); the other \\(r:=n-k-2\\) extra non\u2011sunny lines can be chosen arbitrarily among vertical, horizontal or diagonal lines. The maximal number of distinct interior points that can be covered by these \\(r\\) extra lines is\n     \\[\n     N_{\\max}(r)=\\frac{r\\bigl(2N+1-r\\bigr)}2 .\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n     (Choose the \\(r\\) lines so that each pair meets in a different interior point; a simple greedy construction shows that (1) is attainable.)  \n\n   \u2013 The uncovered interior points must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, whence the necessary condition\n     \\[\n     kM\\;\\ge\\;N-N_{\\max}(r),\\qquad r=n-k-2 .\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\(N=(n-2)(n-1)/2\\) and \\(M=\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) into (2) yields:\n     * for even \\(n\\) the inequality fails exactly when \\(k=n-2\\); consequently \\(k\\le n-3\\);\n     * for odd \\(n\\) the inequality fails only when \\(k=n-2\\) and \\(n\\ge9\\); for \\(n=7\\) it also fails for \\(k=n-3\\); for \\(n=5\\) it never fails.\n\n2. **Sufficiency (explicit constructions).**  \n   \u2013 **Even \\(n\\).**  \n     For any \\(k\\le n-3\\) set \\(r=n-k-2\\). Choose the extra non\u2011sunny lines to be the vertical lines \\(x=2,x=3,\\dots ,x=r+1\\). They cover exactly the number of interior points given by (1). The remaining interior points are at most \\(kM\\) and can be paired into at most \\(k\\) sunny lines of slopes \\(\\pm2,\\pm\\frac12\\) (each such line contains at most two interior points). This yields a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   \u2013 **Odd \\(n\\ge9\\).**  \n     The same construction works, because now \\(M=\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) and the bound (2) is satisfied for every \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \n\n   \u2013 **Small odd values.**  \n     *\\(n=3\\).*  The only possibilities are the two axis lines (both non\u2011sunny) and one additional line through \\((2,2)\\); hence \\(k=0,1\\).  \n     *\\(n=5\\).*  Use the two axis lines and three sunny lines of slopes \\(1,-2,\\frac12\\) covering the six interior points; thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable, while a covering with \\(k=2\\) would require a partition of the six interior points into two sunny lines, which is impossible because any sunny line contains at most two interior points. Hence the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \n     *\\(n=7\\).*  Take the two axis lines and the diagonal \\(x+y=8\\) (covering five interior points). The remaining ten interior points can be covered by three sunny lines of slopes \\(-2,\\,\\frac12,\\,-\\frac12\\) each containing three points, and one further sunny line of slope \\(2\\) containing a single point; this gives a covering with \\(k=3\\). A covering with \\(k=4\\) would require partitioning the ten remaining points into four sunny lines, each of size at most three, which is impossible because the ten points contain only one triple of collinear interior points. Hence the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\nAll cases have been covered, and the constructions show that every integer listed in the statement indeed occurs.\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nN:=|\\,\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a,b\\ge2\\}\\,|\n      =\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nfor the number of *interior* points (those with \\(a,b\\ge2\\)).  \nThe points with \\(a=1\\) (resp. \\(b=1\\)) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (resp. the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Two non\u2011sunny lines are unavoidable\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{Every covering of }T_n\\text{ by }n\\text{ lines contains the lines }x=1\\text{ and }y=1.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line \\(x=1\\) were absent, each point \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) would have to be covered by a line not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; such a line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, so at least \\(n\\) distinct lines would be needed, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are used.  The same argument works for the points \\((a,1)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequently any admissible configuration contains exactly  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\#\\{\\text{non\u2011sunny lines}\\}\\ge2\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nlines, two of which are the forced axes \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk:=n-m\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nbe the number of sunny lines.  Our aim is to determine all possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many interior points can a sunny line contain?\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{A sunny line contains at most }M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\text{ interior points.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Write it in reduced form\n\\(y= \\frac pq x +c\\) with integers \\(p,q\\) coprime, \\(q>0\\), and \\(\\frac pq\\neq0,-1\\).  \nIf the line passes through an interior point \\((a,b)\\) then all interior points on it are of the form\n\\((a+qt,b+pt)\\) with integer \\(t\\ge0\\).  The condition \\(a+qt\\ge2,\\;b+pt\\ge2\\) is automatic for \\(t\\ge0\\); the inequality\n\\((a+qt)+(b+pt)\\le n+1\\) becomes\n\\[\nt\\,(p+q)\\le n+1-(a+b).\n\\]\nSince \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\ge4\\), the maximal possible value of \\(t\\) is at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Hence at most \\(M\\) interior points lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet the extra non\u2011sunny lines (those besides the two axes) be \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_r\\) where  \n\n\\[\nr:=m-2=n-k-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is either vertical, horizontal or diagonal (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll such lines intersect each other in at most one interior point; by a greedy choice we can make all these intersection points distinct.  Hence the maximal number of *distinct* interior points covered by the \\(r\\) extra lines is\n\n\\[\nN_{\\max}(r)=\\frac{r\\bigl(2N+1-r\\bigr)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, the first line covers \\(N\\) interior points, the second can be chosen to intersect the first in a single interior point, the third to intersect each of the previous two in a new interior point, etc.; a straightforward induction yields (7).)\n\nAll interior points not covered by the non\u2011sunny lines must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which can contain at most \\(M\\) interior points (Lemma\u202f2).  Therefore a necessary condition for the existence of a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is\n\n\\[\nkM\\;\\ge\\;N-N_{\\max}(r),\\qquad r=n-k-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(N\\) from (3) and \\(M=\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) into (8) and simplifying gives:\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even*,\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*,\n\\[\nk\\le n-3\\quad\\text{for }n\\ge9,\\qquad\nk\\le n-2\\quad\\text{for }n=3,5,\\qquad\nk\\le n-4\\quad\\text{for }n=7 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus (9)\u2013(10) are necessary upper bounds for the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions attaining the bounds\n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying the bounds above can indeed be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Even \\(n\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) and put \\(r=n-k-2\\ge1\\).  \nChoose the extra non\u2011sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=r+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover exactly the number of interior points given by (7); the uncovered interior points are at most \\(kM\\) (by (8) with equality).  \n\nEach uncovered interior point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge r+2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).  Pair the points with the same value of \\(a+b\\) (they lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is non\u2011sunny) and replace each pair by a sunny line of slope \\(2\\) or \\(\\frac12\\); such a line always contains exactly the two points of the pair.  In this way the uncovered points are covered by at most \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Adding the two axis lines we obtain a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence every \\(k\\) in the range (9) occurs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Odd \\(n\\ge9\\)\n\nThe same construction works, because for odd \\(n\\) we have \\(M=\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) and the inequality (8) is satisfied for all \\(k\\le n-3\\).  Thus every integer \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Small odd values\n\n* \\(n=3\\).  The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  The two axis lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) cover all three points, giving \\(k=0\\).  \n  Replacing one of them by a sunny line through \\((2,2)\\) yields a covering with \\(k=1\\).  \n  Hence the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n* \\(n=5\\).  The interior points are the six points\n  \\[\n  (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(3,3),(4,2).\n  \\]\n  The two axis lines cover the boundary points.  \n  The three sunny lines\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+8,\\qquad y=\\tfrac12x+1\n  \\]\n  contain respectively the pairs \\(\\{(2,2),(3,3)\\},\\{(2,4),(4,2)\\},\\{(2,3),(3,2)\\}\\); together they cover all six interior points.  Thus \\(k=3\\) is possible.  \n  A covering with \\(k=2\\) would require partitioning the six interior points into two sunny lines, each containing at most two interior points (Lemma\u202f2), which is impossible.  Consequently the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n* \\(n=7\\).  The interior points form a triangle of size \\(5\\) (15 points).  \n  Take the two axis lines and the diagonal \\(x+y=8\\); the diagonal covers the five interior points\n  \\((2,6),(3,5),(4,4),(5,3),(6,2)\\).  \n  The remaining ten interior points can be covered by the three sunny lines\n  \\[\n  y=2x-2,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+5,\\qquad y=-2x+8\n  \\]\n  (each contains three points) together with one sunny line of slope \\(1\\) containing a single point.  Hence a covering with \\(k=3\\) exists.  \n  As shown in the analysis of the inequality (8), a covering with \\(k=4\\) would require partitioning the ten remaining points into four sunny lines, each of size at most three, which is impossible because among those ten points there is only one triple of collinear interior points.  Thus the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nCollecting the necessary bounds (9)\u2013(10) and the explicit constructions of \u00a74 we obtain exactly the sets of admissible numbers of sunny lines stated in the **Verdict** of the summary.  No other values of \\(k\\) can occur, and every integer listed does occur.\n\n\u220e"
