ts we obtain inductively\n\n   \\[\n   K_n=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\equiv0\\pmod3,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\equiv1,2\\pmod3 .\n   \\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n   The induction is straightforward: if \\(n\\equiv0\\pmod3\\) then \\(n-3\\equiv0\\pmod3\\) and\n   \\(K_{n-3}=\\{0,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\); adding the four possible values of \\(t\\) yields\n   \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  If \\(n\\equiv1\\) or \\(2\\pmod3\\) then \\(n-3\\equiv n\\pmod3\\); from the\n   induction hypothesis \\(K_{n-3}=\\{0,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\) (or \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-4\\}\\) when\n   \\(n\\equiv2\\pmod3\\)), and adding \\(t\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) gives exactly the set\n   \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\not\\equiv0\\pmod3\\).**  \n   Assume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Then each line meets every side of the\n   triangle in exactly one point; consequently we obtain three permutations\n   \\(\\sigma,\\tau\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) defined by  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i\\cap B_n=\\bigl(i,1\\bigr),\\qquad\n   L_i\\cap L_n=\\bigl(1,\\sigma(i)\\bigr),\\qquad\n   L_i\\cap D_n=\\bigl(\\tau(i),\\,n+1-\\tau(i)\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Collinearity of the three intersection points gives, for every\n   \\(i\\ge2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   (\\sigma(i)-1)\\bigl(i-\\tau(i)\\bigr)=(i-1)\\bigl(n-\\tau(i)\\bigr). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) we obtain after a short manipulation\n\n   \\[\n   i\\bigl(\\sigma(i)-1\\bigr)+(n+1-i)\\bigl(\\tau(i)-1\\bigr)\\equiv0\\pmod n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Summing (2) over all \\(i\\) and using \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}i\\equiv0\\pmod n\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\sigma(i)-1\\bigr)\\equiv\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\tau(i)-1\\bigr)\\equiv0\\pmod n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (3) back into (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}i\\bigl(\\sigma(i)-1\\bigr)\\equiv0\\pmod n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the left‑hand side of (4) is congruent\n   to \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}i^{2}\\pmod n\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}i^{2}\\equiv0\\pmod n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The well‑known formula \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}i^{2}=n(n+1)(2n+1)/6\\) shows that\n   (5) holds **iff** \\(3\\mid n\\).  Consequently, when \\(n\\not\\equiv0\\pmod3\\) a\n   configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist.\n\n   This proves that for \\(n\\equiv1,2\\pmod3\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible,\n   while for \\(n\\equiv0\\pmod3\\) the construction of Lemma 1 with \\(k'=n-3\\) and\n   \\(t=3\\) yields a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\nCombining the constructions, the recursive step, the base cases and the\nimpossibility lemma gives the complete description of the admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nK_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\equiv0\\pmod3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\equiv1\\text{ or }2\\pmod3 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThus the answer to the problem is exactly the set displayed above.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
