ith \\(1\\le j\\le q\\) the vertical line \\(L^{V}_{j}\\) contains the points \\((j,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\).  \nAmong them the points belonging to the already covered diagonals are exactly those with\n\\(j+b=n+1-i\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{0,\\dots ,q-1\\}\\); there are \\(\\min\\{q,n-j+1\\}\\) such points.  \nConsequently the number of **new** points contributed by column \\(j\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n(n+1-j)-\\min\\{q,n-j+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSumming over \\(j=1,\\dots ,q\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{q}\\bigl[(n+1-j)-\\min\\{q,n-j+1\\}\\bigr]\n   =\\frac{(n-q+1)(n-q)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(r=1\\) the extra vertical line \\(x=q+1\\) contributes additionally  \n\n\\[\nn-q\\quad\\text{new points}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAdding (11), (12) and, when needed, (13) we obtain the total number of points covered by the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\#\\text{covered}\n&=qn-\\frac{q(q-1)}2+\\frac{(n-q+1)(n-q)}2+(r)(n-q)\\\\[2mm]\n&=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus exactly  \n\n\\[\nU:=2k\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remain uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Geometry of the uncovered points\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\) is uncovered precisely when\n\n\\[\na\\ge q+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n-q+1 .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nConsequences of (16):\n\n* No two uncovered points share the same column, because the column index \\(a\\) is forced to be distinct (it must be at least \\(q+1\\) and the inequality \\(a+b\\le n-q+1\\) leaves at most one admissible \\(b\\) for a given \\(a\\)).  \n* No two uncovered points share the same row, because the row index \\(b\\) is determined uniquely by the column via \\(b\\le n-q+1-a\\).  \n* No two uncovered points lie on a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\), because (16) forces the sum \\(a+b\\) to be at most \\(n-q+1\\) and each admissible sum occurs for at most one point.\n\nHence any two distinct uncovered points have different \\(x\\)‑coordinates, different \\(y\\)‑coordinates and different sums \\(x+y\\). Their connecting line therefore has a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Adding the sunny lines\n\nSince \\(U=2k\\) by (15), the uncovered points can be partitioned into \\(k\\) unordered pairs.  \nFor each pair draw the unique line through its two points; by the observation above this line is sunny.  \nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct (each contains a different pair) and together they cover all previously uncovered points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.4  Summary of the construction\n\nWe have produced:\n\n* \\(m=n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines (the \\(q\\) diagonals (8) and the \\(q+r\\) vertical lines (9)–(10));\n* \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through a distinct pair of the remaining points.\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  \nThus a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (6) derived from the counting argument is also sufficient, as shown by the explicit construction. Therefore the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n\\,(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n∎"
