with\nexactly one non\u2011sunny line is impossible.  The same argument works when\nthe non\u2011sunny line is vertical or diagonal.  Hence for odd \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The case \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nand the \\(k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=k .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-k\\) (hence lies on\n\\(y=b\\)) or \\(a>n-k\\); the latter inequality forces \\(b\\le k\\) and the\npoint lies on \\(x=b\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) lines (14)\u2013(15) cover all points.\n\nFor each vertical line \\(x=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le k\\)) the unique point not\nalready covered by a horizontal line is \\((n-k+j,j)\\).\nReplace the vertical line \\(x=j\\) by the sunny line \\(L_j\\) defined as\n\n\\[\nL_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{the line through }(n-k+1,1)\\text{ and }(1,2), & j=1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{the line through }(n-k+j,j)\\text{ and }(1,1), & j\\ge2 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(j\\ge2\\) the slope equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{j-1}{\\,n-k+j-1\\,}>0\\);\nfor \\(j=1\\) the slope is \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-k\\,}\\).  In all cases the slope\ndiffers from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\nThe lines \\(L_j\\) are pairwise distinct because their slopes are distinct.\nAll points of the former vertical line \\(x=j\\) remain covered:\nthe points with \\(a\\le n-k\\) are still on the horizontal line \\(y=a\\),\nand the point \\((n-k+j,j)\\) is now on \\(L_j\\).\nHence we obtain a configuration of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=n-1\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nLet \\(n=2t\\) be even.\nStart with the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lin