y that relies on it is on shaky ground.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Maximal size of a non‑sunny line  \n\nThe analysis of horizontal, vertical, and diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)) lines is correct: each can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). No error here.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Upper bounds for \\(k\\)\n\nThe inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\ge|T_n|\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nis derived from the (unproved) bound for sunny lines. Assuming (10) were true, the algebra leading to (12) is correct, giving  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1 & (n\\text{ even}),\\\\\nn   & (n\\text{ odd}).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus (12) is conditionally valid, but its foundation is the faulty bound (10).  \n\n**Sharpening for odd \\(n\\) (13).**  \nThe author argues: “If exactly one line is non‑sunny and it is horizontal \\(y=r\\), the remaining points form the triangle \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) (all points with \\(b\\ge r+1\\)).” This statement is false. After removing the horizontal line \\(y=r\\) the points with \\(b<r\\) are **still present** and must be covered by the sunny lines. The set of uncovered points is not the triangle \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\); its size is larger than \\(|T_{\\,n-1}|\\). Consequently the counting argument that leads to the inequality \\(\\frac{(n-1)^2}{2}<\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) is invalid. No rigorous proof is given that a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line cannot exist.  \n**Classification:** Critical Error.\n\nBecause the proof of (13) is invalid, the claimed restriction \\(k\\le n-2\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Constructions  \n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nThe construction starts with \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) and \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\). This indeed covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe next step replaces each vertical line \\(x=j\\) by a sunny line \\(L_j\\) defined in (16).  \n\n*For \\(j\\ge2\\):* \\(L_j\\) passes through \\((n-k+j,j)\\) and \\((1,1)\\