   Thus all four values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) occur.\n\nCombining the upper bound (2) with the explicit constructions above yields the complete description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line and let \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\((x_1,y_1)\\neq(x_2,y_2)\\).  \nSince the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is not \\(-1\\), the sums \\(s_i:=x_i+y_i\\) are distinct; otherwise the segment joining the two points would have slope \\(-1\\).  \nHence each point of \\(\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\) yields a distinct integer sum \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \nThe number of such sums is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), because the sums come in pairs \\((s,n+2-s)\\) and at most one element of each pair can be realised on a line of slope different from \\(-1\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=t\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in the points \\((a,t)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\); the latter interval has length at most \\(n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=s\\).  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\); intersecting with \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) gives the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\le n+1-a\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n\\).  Hence at most \\(n\\) points occur. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Counting inequality\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nkM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|\n   \\;=\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor=n-M\\), inequality (10) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy (11).  \nFor \\(n=3\\) inequality (11) gives \\(k\\le2\\); a direct inspection (see § 3) shows that \\(k=3\\) is also possible, so the case \\(n=3\\) will be treated separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for all admissible \\(k\\) ( \\(n\\ge4\\) )\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nv:=\\max\\{0,m-k\\},\\qquad h:=m-v .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(v+h=m=n-k\\) and \\(v,h\\ge0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1 Sunny lines\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=x+d_i,\\qquad d_i:=-(k-i)\\;(=-(k-1),\\dots ,0).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny and pairwise distinct.\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\in\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,0\\}\\) iff it lies on one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  Consequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\ncovers exactly those points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose difference \\(b-a\\) belongs to the interval \\([-(k-1),0]\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2 Non‑sunny lines\n\nAdd the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,v)\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,h).\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny, they are distinct, and their total number is \\(v+h=m=n-k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3 Every point is covered\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If* \\(b-a\\in\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,0\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal S\\) and the point lies on a sunny line.\n\n*Otherwise* either \\(b-a\\ge1\\) or \\(b-a\\le -k\\).\n\n*Case* \\(b-a\\ge1\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le h\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \nIf \\(b>h\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-h=v,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Case* \\(b-a\\le -k\\).  \nInterchanging the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) reduces this to the previous case, and the same argument shows that \\((a,b)\\) is covered by a line from the families (16)–(17).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.  The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the \\(V_j\\) and \\(H_j\\)).  Hence a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (12).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nFour explicit families of three distinct lines are:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\nk=0:&\\{y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3\\},\\\\[1mm]\nk=1:&\\{y=x,\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\},\\\\[1mm]\nk=2:&\\{y=x,\\;y=x+1,\\;x=1\\},\\\\[1mm]\nk=3:&\\{y=x,\\;y=x+1,\\;y=\\tfrac12x\\}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nEach family covers all six points, and the numbers of sunny lines are respectively \\(0,1,2,3\\).  Hence for \\(n=3\\) all four values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) inequality (11) is both necessary and sufficient; together with the explicit constructions of § 2 we obtain that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\]\n\nFor the small case \\(n=3\\) all four possibilities \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are realizable.  This completes the determination of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists."
