ls \\(nM\\).)\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (3) and put \\(m:=n-k\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   Choose the \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(1\\)  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=x+d_i,\\qquad\n   d_i:=-(k-i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k),\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   i.e. the intercepts run through \\(\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,0\\}\\).  \n   Each \\(L_i\\) contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-d_i}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); together they cover all points whose difference \\(b-a\\) lies in \\([-(k-1),0]\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   Write \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   If \\(m\\le k\\) we take only horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,m).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(m>k\\) we take \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,k\\) and the remaining \\(m-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,m-k).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in (5)\u2013(6) are non\u2011sunny, distinct, and their total number is \\(m=n-k\\).\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   - If \\(b-a\\in\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,0\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\).\n   - Otherwise \\(b-a\\ge1\\) or \\(b-a\\le -k\\).\n\n     + If \\(b-a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\le k\\) (or \\(b\\le m\\) when \\(m\\le k\\)) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) from (5) or (6).\n     \n     + If \\(b-a\\ge1\\) and \\(b>k\\) (the case \\(m>k\\)) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le m-k\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (6).\n\n     + The symmetric situation \\(b-a\\le -k\\) is handled by exchanging the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\); the same families of horizontal/vertical lines cover the point.\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.  The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, proving that every integer \\(k\\) satis