ntal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,m).\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   - If \\(m>k\\) we take the \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,k\\) and the remaining\n     \\(m-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,m-k).\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n\n   All lines in (5)–(6) are non‑sunny, distinct, and their total number is \\(m=n-k\\).\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   - If \\(b-a\\in\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,0\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{b-a+k}\\) from (4).\n\n   - Otherwise \\(b-a\\ge1\\) or \\(b-a\\le -k\\).\n\n     + If \\(b-a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\le k\\) (or \\(b\\le m\\) when \\(m\\le k\\)) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) from (5) or (6).\n\n     + If \\(b-a\\ge1\\) and \\(b>k\\) (the case \\(m>k\\)) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n       \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le m-k\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (6).\n\n     + The symmetric situation \\(b-a\\le -k\\) is handled by exchanging the roles of\n       \\(a\\) and \\(b\\); the same families of horizontal/vertical lines cover the point.\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\n   proving that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) is attainable.\n\n5. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the set \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) consists of six points.  Direct inspection\n   yields families of three distinct lines with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) sunny lines, so all four\n   values occur.  For \\(n=4\\) the bound (3) gives \\(k\\le3\\); the construction above\n   with \\(k=3\\) (three sunny lines of slope 1 and one horizontal line) works, confirming\n   the bound is sharp.\n\nCombining the necessary bound (3) with the explicit constructions gives the complete\ncharacterisation of admissible numbers of sunny lines stated at the beginning. ∎