tisfy the congruence \\(p a\\equiv -qd\\pmod q\\); the admissible abscissas form an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(|q|\\).  \n   Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the largest possible number of admissible \\(a\\)’s is\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\), this quantity does not exceed \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=M\\).\n\n   *Irrational slope.*  \n   If the slope is irrational, the line contains at most one lattice point, and \\(1\\le M\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Hence (1) holds for all sunny lines. ∎\n\n2. **Lemma 2 (points on a non‑sunny line).**  \n   A horizontal line \\(y=t\\), a vertical line \\(x=s\\) or a line of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M\\) points, while each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma 2). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and solving (2) for \\(k\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For odd \\(n\\) the inequality is automatically satisfied because\n   \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2=n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (3) and put \\(m:=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   Choose the \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(1\\)  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=x-(i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) the line \\(L_i\\) contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n   points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); together they cover all points whose difference\n   \\(b-a\\) lies in the interval \\([-(k-1),0]\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Write \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   We use two families of non‑sunny lines:\n\n   - vertical lines \\(V_j:\\;x=j\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\);\n   - horizontal lines \\(H_j:\\;y=j\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor+\\lceil m/2\\rceil=m\\), as required, and all of them are non‑sunny.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   1. **Case \\(b-a\\in\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,0\\}\\).**  \n      Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{b-a+k}\\) from (4).\n\n   2. **Case \\(b-a\\ge 1\\).**  \n      From \\(a+b\\le n+1=n-k+k+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(k+1)=m\\).  \n      Hence \\(a\\le m\\).  \n      If \\(a\\le\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\);  \n      otherwise \\(a>\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\) and therefore \\(a\\le m\\) implies\n      \\(b\\le m\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>m+m=n-k+n-k\\ge n+1\\), a contradiction).  \n      In this sub‑case \\(b\\le\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line\n      \\(H_b\\).\n\n   3. **Case \\(b-a\\le -(k)\\).**  \n      This is symmetric to the previous case after swapping the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\);\n      the same families of vertical and horizontal lines cover the point.\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)’s) and \\(m=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\n   proving that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) is attainable.\n\n5. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the set \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) consists of six points.  One checks directly that\n   three distinct lines can be chosen with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) sunny lines, so all four values occur.\n   For 