lution ###\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Existence for \\(k=0\\)).  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the \\(n\\) vertical lines\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\n\\]\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and together they contain every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach line \\(x=i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is not sunny by definition.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Existence for \\(k=n\\)).  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the \\(n\\) lines\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\Bigl(i-\\tfrac12\\Bigr)x + (n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\nare distinct, each of them is sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n1. **Distinctness and sunniness.**  \n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i-\\tfrac12\\). For any integer \\(i\\) this slope is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), nor \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   If \\(i\\neq j\\) then the slopes differ, so the lines are distinct.\n\n2. **Covering property.**  \n   Fix a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Consider the function\n   \\[\n   f(i)=\\Bigl(i-\\tfrac12\\Bigr)a + (n+1-i)-b\n        = a\\Bigl(i-\\tfrac12\\Bigr) - i + (n+1-b).\n   \\]\n   This is an affine function of the integer variable \\(i\\).  \n   Compute its values at the endpoints:\n   \\[\n   f(1)=\\frac{a}{2}+ (n+1-b)-1,\\qquad\n   f(n)=\\bigl(n-\\tfrac12\\bigr)a + (n+1-n)-b\n        =\\bigl(n-\\tfrac12\\bigr)a +1-b .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\) while \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), one checks that\n   \\[\n   f(1)\\le 0\\le f(n).\n   \\]\n   (Indeed, \\(f(1)\\le 0\\) is equivalent to \\(a\\le 2b-2\\), which holds when \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\); similarly \\(f(n)\\ge 0\\).)  \n   Since