 verify that every required lattice point lies on at least one of them.\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (many sunny lines).**  \n   We start from the construction of Lemma\u202f1 and replace, one by one, some of the non\u2011sunny lines by *generic* sunny lines while preserving the covering property.  \n   The key observation is that each point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at most three of the lines of Lemma\u202f1 (one of each direction). Hence, after we have already covered a point, we may delete any one of the three lines that passes through it and replace it by a new sunny line that passes through a *different* uncovered point.  \n   By iterating this replacement we can increase the number of sunny lines arbitrarily, up to the total number \\(n\\) of lines. The process stops when we have replaced all non\u2011sunny lines, which yields the configuration with \\(k=n\\).  \n   The construction works as long as we have at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines, because the initial configuration of Lemma\u202f1 uses at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) non\u2011sunny lines; therefore we can replace each of them and obtain any intermediate value of \\(k\\) between \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of intermediate values.**  \n   Suppose a configuration contains \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines. Each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) of the required points (the whole set has \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points). A counting argument shows that if \\(s\\ge 1\\) then necessarily \\(s\\ge \\lceil n/2\\rceil\\). Consequently the number of sunny lines \\(k=n-s\\) cannot lie strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil-1\\).\n\nPutting the three parts together yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThus a non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or of the form \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Existence of a covering with no sunny line.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH&=\\{y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\},\\\\[2pt]\nV&=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\},\\\\[2pt]\nD&=\\{x+y=c\\mid c=n+2-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(H\\cup V\\cup D\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nWe claim that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=H\\cup V\\cup D\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Count of lines.*  \n\\(|H|=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), \\(|V|=\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), \\(|D|= \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_0|=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+\\lceil n/2\\rceil+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=n .\n\\]\n\n*Covering property.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in H\\) contains the point.  \nOtherwise \\(b>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=n+2-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a\\) belongs to the interval \\([1,\\,n+2-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor]\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\in V\\) contains the point.  \nIf \\(a>\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) then \\(a+b\\ge \\lceil n/2\\rceil+1\\) and, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na+b\\in\\{n+2-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor,\\dots ,n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nso the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\in D\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2.  From \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) up to \\(n\\) sunny lines are possible.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) be the configuration of Lemma\u202f1.  \nIt contains  \n\n\\[\ns_0:=|\\mathcal L_0\\cap\\{\\text{non\u2011sunny lines}\\}|=|\\mathcal L_0|=n\n\\]\n\nnon\u2011sunny lines and zero sunny lines.  \nObserve that each point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to **exactly three** lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\): one horizontal, one vertical and one diagonal (the three families are defined so that they intersect pairwise in the whole triangle).\n\nWe now perform the following replacement operation:\n\n> **Replacement step.**  \n> Choose a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) that is still present.  \n> Pick a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) that is covered *only* by \\(\\ell\\) (such a point exists because each point is covered by three lines, and after any number of replacements at most two of them can have been removed).  \n> Remove \\(\\ell\\) and insert a new line \\(\\ell'\\) that passes through \\((a,b)\\) and has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (hence \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny).  \n> The new line \\(\\ell'\\) does not coincide with any existing line because its slope is new.\n\nAfter the step the total number of lines stays \\(n\\); the number of sunny lines increases by one and the number of non\u2011sunny lines decreases by one.  \nMoreover the covering property is preserved: the point \\((a,b)\\) is still covered (now by \\(\\ell'\\)), and all other points keep at least one of their former covering lines.\n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) we can apply the replacement step repeatedly.  \nEach application reduces the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one, so after \\(t\\) steps we obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{sunny lines}=t,\\qquad\n\\#\\text{non\u2011sunny lines}=n-t .\n\\]\n\nThe process can continue as long as there remains a non\u2011sunny line, i.e. while \\(t\\le n-1\\).  \nConsequently we can realise every integer  \n\n\\[\nk=t\\quad\\text{with}\\quad 0\\le t\\le n .\n\\]\n\nHowever, the first replacement step requires the existence of a point that is covered **only** by a non\u2011sunny line.  \nIn the initial configuration \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) such points are precisely those lying on the extreme rows or columns of the triangle; there are \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) of them.  \nThus the first \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) replacements are always possible, giving configurations with  \n\n\\[\nk=0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nAfter these \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) steps every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least two remaining non\u2011sunny lines, so a further replacement would need a point covered by exactly one non\u2011sunny line.  \nSuch a point exists precisely when the number of remaining non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  \nHence we can continue the process until the number of non\u2011sunny lines drops to \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), i.e. until the number of sunny lines reaches  \n\n\\[\nk=n-\\lceil n/2\\rceil=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFrom that moment on, each remaining non\u2011sunny line can be eliminated one by one, because after we have at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) non\u2011sunny lines, every point of the triangle lies on at most two of them, guaranteeing the existence of a point covered by a single non\u2011sunny line.  \nThus we can increase \\(k\\) all the way up to \\(n\\).\n\nCollecting the attainable values we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\lceil n/2\\rceil,\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of intermediate values\n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(s\\ge 1\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nEach non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\): a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) meets the triangle in at most \\(n\\) integer points, and the same bound holds for vertical and diagonal lines.  \nHence the total number of incidences between points of \\(T_n\\) and non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(sn\\).\n\nOn the other hand, by (1) the set \\(T_n\\) has \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points, and every point must lie on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nIf a point lies on a sunny line, it does not contribute to the count \\(sn\\).  \nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nsn\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;-\\;(\\text{number of points covered only by sunny lines})\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n .\n\\]\n\nSince the right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge \\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the number of sunny lines \\(k=n-s\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil = \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus any configuration with at least one non\u2011sunny line can have at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\) cannot lie in the interval \\([1,\\lceil n/2\\rceil-1]\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f1, Lemma\u202f2 and the impossibility argument, the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\bigr\\}} .\n\\]"
