es?\n\n   If the answer is “yes”, then for any \\(n\\ge k\\) we obtain a covering of \\(T_n\\) by adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).\n\n4. **Capacity bound for a sunny line.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line meets \\(T_k\\) in at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  \n   Proof: a sunny line is not parallel to any side of the triangle, so it meets the triangle in a segment whose integer points form an arithmetic progression of step equal to the primitive direction vector; the length of the segment is at most \\(k\\), giving the bound.\n\n   Consequently  \n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Even \\(k\\) are impossible.**  \n   If \\(k=2m\\) then \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor=m\\) and (1) becomes \\(2m^2\\ge2m^2+m\\), a contradiction.  \n   Hence **no even \\(k\\ge2\\) can occur**.\n\n6. **Odd \\(k\\ge5\\) are impossible.**  \n   Let \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).  \n   *Lemma 2.* A sunny line can contain the maximal number \\(m+1=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) of points of \\(T_k\\) only if its slope is \\(1\\) (the line \\(y=x\\)) or \\(-2\\) (the line \\(y=-2x+2m+3\\)). Thus there are at most **two** such lines.  \n\n   To achieve equality in (1) for odd \\(k\\) we must have every sunny line contain exactly \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points; otherwise the total number of covered points would be smaller than \\(|T_k|\\). Since we need \\(k\\ge5\\) distinct lines each with \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points but at most two lines can have that many points, a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible.  \n\n   Hence **odd \\(k\\ge5\\) are impossible**.\n\n7. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). All are non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\). Add any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) (e.g. \\(y=2x-(2n-1)\\)).  \n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\). The uncovered points form \\(T_3\\). The following three sunny lines cover \\(T_3\\) (after shifting the \\(x\\)–coordinate by \\(n-3\\)):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: y = x - n + 3,\\\\\n   \\ell_2 &: y = -\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{n+2}{2},\\\\\n   \\ell_3 &: y = -2x + 2n - 1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Their slopes are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); each line contains exactly two of the remaining points, and together they cover all points of \\(T_3\\). Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines yields a covering of \\(T_n\\) with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n   Thus \\(k=0,1,3\\) are always attainable.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary conditions (steps 5–6) leave only \\(k=0,1,3\\); the constructions of step 7 show that each of these values is indeed attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\text{ and }3.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
